// History — Asia & the wider world (UPSC GS-I world history & India's external links).
// Group 'his-world'. Pages: his-islamic, his-mongols, his-silk, his-greaterindia, his-buddhism, his-worldhist.

/* ======================= ISLAMIC WORLD ======================= */
GEO.add({
 id:'his-islamic', group:'his-world', title:'Islamic World: Caliphates & Empires', bn:'ইসলামি বিশ্ব: খিলাফত ও সাম্রাজ্য', icon:'🕌', world:true,
 intro:'রাশিদুন → উমাইয়া (দামাস্কাস) → আব্বাসীয় (বাগদাদ) → ফাতিমীয় (কায়রো) → অটোমান (ইস্তাম্বুল) — ক্রমটা মনে রাখো; গজনি, ঘুর, কাবুল, ফারগানা থেকেই ভারতে আক্রমণ এসেছিল।',
 types:{caliph:['Caliphate capital','#2e7d32'], dyn:['Dynasty / sultanate capital','#1565c0'], holy:['Holy city / shrine','#f9a825'], battle:['Battle','#c62828']},
 pts:[
  ['Mecca — Kaaba',21.423,39.826,'Saudi Arabia','Birthplace of Prophet Muhammad (c. 570 CE); Kaaba; Hijra to Medina in 622 CE begins the Hijri calendar; Hajj.','holy',
   { old: 'Makkah; Bakkah (Quranic name); Umm al-Qura ("mother of towns")',
     more: 'Mecca was a caravan and pilgrimage town of the Quraysh tribe on the Red Sea incense route. Muhammad was born here c. 570 and began preaching c. 610; persecution led to the Hijra to Medina in 622 (year 1 of the Hijri calendar). He took Mecca peacefully in 630 and cleared the Kaaba of idols. The Kaaba inside the Masjid al-Haram is the qibla (prayer direction) and centre of the Hajj, one of the five pillars of Islam.' } ],
  ['Medina — Masjid an-Nabawi',24.468,39.611,'Saudi Arabia','City of the Prophet after the Hijra (622); capital of the first Rashidun caliphs until Ali moved to Kufa (656).','caliph',
   { old: 'Yathrib (pre-Islamic) → Madinat an-Nabi ("City of the Prophet"); Taybah',
     more: 'Oasis town of Yathrib renamed Medina after the Prophet migrated here in 622. The Constitution of Medina organised the first Muslim community (ummah). The Masjid an-Nabawi, built by Muhammad, holds his tomb under the Green Dome. Medina was capital of Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman until Ali shifted to Kufa in 656. It is Islam\'s second holiest city.' } ],
  ['Badr (624)',23.78,38.7906,'Saudi Arabia','First major battle of Islam: Muslims of Medina defeat the Quraysh of Mecca, 624 CE.','battle',
   { old: 'Badr Hunayn (modern town name)',
     more: 'At the wells of Badr, south-west of Medina, c. 313 Muslims led by Muhammad defeated a much larger Meccan (Quraysh) force under Abu Jahl in March 624 (17 Ramadan, 2 AH). Abu Jahl was killed. The victory strengthened Muhammad\'s position in Medina and is regarded as the first decisive battle of Islam; it was followed by Uhud (625) and the Trench (627).' } ],
  ['Jerusalem — Dome of the Rock',31.778,35.235,'Israel / Palestine','Dome of the Rock built by Umayyad Abd al-Malik (691); Al-Aqsa; Crusader kingdom 1099–1187.','holy',
   { old: 'Aelia Capitolina (Roman); Al-Quds / Bayt al-Maqdis (Arabic)',
     more: 'Jerusalem was taken by Caliph Umar\'s Arabs in 637/638. The Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik built the Dome of the Rock (completed 691/692) over the rock on the Temple Mount (Haram al-Sharif), linked by tradition to the Prophet\'s Night Journey (Isra and Miraj); it is the oldest surviving major Islamic monument. The First Crusade captured the city in 1099; Saladin retook it in 1187. Holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims.' } ],
  ['Hattin (1187)',32.804,35.444,'Israel (Galilee)','Saladin (Ayyubid) crushes the Crusaders, 1187; Jerusalem retaken; provokes the Third Crusade.','battle',
   { old: 'Horns of Hattin (Qarne Hittin)',
     more: 'On 4 July 1187 Saladin (Salah ad-Din), founder of the Ayyubid dynasty, trapped and destroyed the thirsty army of the Crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem under Guy of Lusignan near the twin hills of Hattin above Lake Tiberias. The True Cross relic was captured and Reynald of Châtillon executed. Saladin took Jerusalem on 2 October 1187, which provoked the Third Crusade (Richard I, Philip II, Frederick Barbarossa).' } ],
  ['Yarmuk (636)',32.814,35.955,'Syria–Jordan border','Rashidun Arabs under Khalid ibn al-Walid defeat the Byzantines, 636; Syria passes to Islam.','battle',
   { old: 'Hieromyax (Greek name of the Yarmuk river)',
     more: 'In August 636, on the Yarmuk river (a tributary of the Jordan), the Rashidun Arab army under Khalid ibn al-Walid, serving Caliph Umar, defeated a large army of Byzantine emperor Heraclius after six days of fighting. The battle ended Byzantine rule in Syria and Palestine; Jerusalem fell soon after (637/638). With Qadisiyyah (636) against Persia, it marks the explosive early Arab conquests.' } ],
  ['Damascus — Umayyad Mosque',33.511,36.307,'Syria','Umayyad caliphate capital (661–750) from Muawiya; Great Umayyad Mosque completed c. 715.','caliph',
   { old: 'Dimashq; Al-Sham; Roman temple of Jupiter → Basilica of St John the Baptist on the mosque site',
     more: 'Muawiya I, governor of Syria, founded the Umayyad caliphate in 661 with Damascus as capital; it lasted until the Abbasid revolution of 750. Caliph al-Walid I built the Great Umayyad Mosque (c. 706–715) on the site of a Christian basilica, decorated with Byzantine-style mosaics; it shelters a shrine of John the Baptist. Saladin\'s tomb lies beside it. The Old City is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.' } ],
  ['Karbala (680)',32.615,44.031,'Iraq','Husain ibn Ali killed by Umayyad Yazid’s army, 680; mourned at Muharram (Ashura); chief Shia shrine.','holy',
   { old: 'Karbala al-Muʿalla; Ninawa / Taff (old names of the battlefield)',
     more: 'On 10 Muharram (Ashura) 680, Husain ibn Ali, grandson of the Prophet, and his small band of family and followers were killed by the army of the Umayyad caliph Yazid I at Karbala on the Euphrates. The martyrdom became the defining event of Shia Islam, commemorated during Muharram with processions and tazias (also in India). The Imam Husayn Shrine and the shrine of Abbas ibn Ali are major pilgrimage centres (Arbaeen).' } ],
  ['Kufa',32.029,44.401,'Iraq','Arab garrison city (638); Caliph Ali’s capital (656–661); first Abbasid seat (749–762); Kufic script.','caliph',
   { old: 'Al-Kufah (Arab misr / garrison town)',
     more: 'Founded c. 638 as an Arab garrison city (misr) after Qadisiyyah, Kufa became the capital of the fourth caliph Ali (656–661), who was assassinated in its Great Mosque in 661; he is buried in nearby Najaf. It was a centre of pro-Alid (Shia) support and the first seat of the Abbasids (749) before Baghdad. The angular Kufic script, used for early Quran copies, is named after it.' } ],
  ['Al-Qadisiyyah (636)',31.583,44.5,'Iraq','Arabs defeat Sasanian Persians, c. 636; opens Iraq & Iran to Islam; Ctesiphon falls soon after.','battle',
   { old: 'Qadisiya (near Hira, south-west of Kufa)',
     more: 'The Rashidun army under Sa\'d ibn Abi Waqqas defeated the Sasanian Persian army led by general Rustam Farrokhzad (killed in battle) at Qadisiyyah, near the Euphrates, c. 636 (dates 636–637 given). The Arabs then took the Sasanian capital Ctesiphon (637); Nahavand (642) finished the Sasanian empire, and its last shah Yazdegerd III died in 651. Persia was gradually Islamised, and Zoroastrian Parsis later migrated to Gujarat.' } ],
  ['Baghdad — Round City',33.348,44.335,'Iraq','Abbasid capital founded by al-Mansur (762); House of Wisdom; sacked by Hulagu 1258, ending the Abbasid caliphate.','caliph',
   { old: 'Madinat al-Salam ("City of Peace", official Abbasid name)',
     more: 'Caliph al-Mansur founded the circular walled city of Madinat al-Salam on the Tigris in 762. Under Harun al-Rashid and al-Mamun, Baghdad became the world\'s leading centre of learning; the Bayt al-Hikma (House of Wisdom) translated Greek, Persian and Sanskrit works (e.g. Indian numerals, Brahmagupta\'s astronomy). The Mongol Hulagu sacked it in 1258 and executed caliph al-Mustasim, ending the Abbasid caliphate in Baghdad. Nothing of the Round City survives.' } ],
  ['Samarra',34.206,43.88,'Iraq','Abbasid capital 836–892 (al-Mutasim); spiral Malwiya minaret of the Great Mosque; UNESCO.','caliph',
   { old: 'Surra man ra\'a ("delighted is he who sees it", Abbasid name)',
     more: 'Caliph al-Mutasim moved the Abbasid capital from Baghdad to Samarra in 836 to house his Turkish slave soldiers (ghilman); it remained capital until 892. Al-Mutawakkil built the Great Mosque (848–852), once the world\'s largest, with the 52 m spiral Malwiya minaret. Samarra also holds the al-Askari Shrine of the 10th and 11th Shia imams (bombed 2006). The Samarra Archaeological City is a UNESCO site (2007).' } ],
  ['Córdoba — Mezquita',37.879,-4.78,'Spain','Capital of Umayyad emirate/caliphate of al-Andalus (756–1031); Great Mosque begun by Abd al-Rahman I (785).','caliph',
   { old: 'Corduba (Roman, capital of Hispania Baetica); Qurtuba (Arabic)',
     more: 'Abd al-Rahman I, an Umayyad prince who escaped the Abbasid massacre of 750, founded the emirate of Córdoba in 756 and began the Great Mosque in 785 on the site of a Visigothic church. Abd al-Rahman III proclaimed a caliphate in 929, and Córdoba became the largest, most cultured city of western Europe (Averroes and Maimonides were born here). Ferdinand III took it in 1236; a cathedral was later built inside the mosque. UNESCO 1984.' } ],
  ['Granada — Alhambra',37.177,-3.59,'Spain','Nasrid palace; Granada, last Muslim state in Spain, fell to Ferdinand & Isabella in 1492 (end of Reconquista).','dyn',
   { old: 'Gharnata (Arabic); Alhambra = al-Qal\'a al-Hamra ("the Red Fort")',
     more: 'The Nasrid dynasty (1238–1492) ruled the Emirate of Granada, the last Muslim state of al-Andalus. The Alhambra palace-fortress, mainly built under Yusuf I and Muhammad V in the 14th century, is famous for the Court of the Lions and muqarnas stucco. Muhammad XII (Boabdil) surrendered Granada to Ferdinand and Isabella on 2 January 1492, completing the Reconquista; the same year Columbus sailed. Alhambra, Generalife and Albayzín are a UNESCO site.' } ],
  ['Tours–Poitiers (732)',46.781,0.541,'France','Charles Martel’s Franks halt the Umayyad advance into Western Europe, 732.','battle',
   { old: 'Battle of Poitiers / Balat al-Shuhada ("Pavement of Martyrs" in Arab sources)',
     more: 'In October 732 (sometimes dated 733), the Frankish leader Charles Martel defeated an Umayyad army under Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi, governor of al-Andalus, between Tours and Poitiers; al-Ghafiqi was killed. It marked the high-water mark of the Arab advance into western Europe and boosted the Carolingian family; Charles Martel\'s grandson was Charlemagne. Its "saving Europe" importance is debated by modern historians.' } ],
  ['Kairouan — Great Mosque',35.681,10.104,'Tunisia','Founded by Uqba ibn Nafi (670); Aghlabid capital; Mosque of Uqba; holiest city of the Maghreb; UNESCO.','dyn',
   { old: 'Al-Qayrawan ("caravan camp"); Ifriqiya capital',
     more: 'Uqba ibn Nafi founded Kairouan in 670 as the Arab base for the conquest of North Africa (Ifriqiya). The Great Mosque (Mosque of Uqba), largely rebuilt by the Aghlabids in the 9th century, has the oldest standing minaret in the Muslim world and served as a model for Maghrebi mosques. Kairouan was capital of the Aghlabids (800–909) and early Fatimids, and is considered the fourth holiest city of Sunni Islam. UNESCO 1988.' } ],
  ['Cairo — Al-Azhar',30.046,31.263,'Egypt','Fatimid (Shia) caliphate capital founded 969; Al-Azhar (970–72) among oldest universities; later Mamluk capital.','caliph',
   { old: 'Al-Qahira ("the Victorious", Fatimid 969); earlier Fustat (641) & Babylon Fortress',
     more: 'The Arab general Amr ibn al-As founded Fustat in 641. The Shia Ismaili Fatimids conquered Egypt in 969 and their general Jawhar founded al-Qahira as caliphal capital; Al-Azhar Mosque (970–972) became a university and today is the leading centre of Sunni learning. Saladin ended the Fatimid caliphate in 1171. Cairo was later capital of the Mamluk sultanate (1250–1517), which hosted a shadow Abbasid caliphate after 1258. Historic Cairo is UNESCO-listed.' } ],
  ['Ain Jalut (1260)',32.551,35.357,'Israel (Jezreel Valley)','Mamluks under Qutuz & Baybars defeat the Mongols, 1260 — first decisive check on Mongol expansion.','battle',
   { old: 'Ayn Jalut ("Spring of Goliath"); Harod Spring (Ein Harod) in Hebrew',
     more: 'On 3 September 1260 the Mamluk sultan Qutuz and general Baybars defeated a Mongol army under Kitbuqa (Hulagu\'s commander) at Ain Jalut in the Jezreel Valley; Kitbuqa was killed. Hulagu had withdrawn most troops after the death of Great Khan Möngke. It was the first major defeat of the Mongols in a pitched battle, saving Egypt and Islam\'s heartland; Baybars soon killed Qutuz and became sultan. Hand cannons were reportedly used.' } ],
  ['Istanbul — Hagia Sophia',41.008,28.98,'Turkey','Constantinople taken by Ottoman Mehmed II, 1453 (end of Byzantium); Ottoman capital till 1922.','dyn',
   { old: 'Byzantium (Greek colony) → Constantinople (330, Constantine) → Istanbul (official 1930)',
     more: 'Constantine I made Byzantium his new capital, Constantinople, in 330. Justinian built Hagia Sophia (532–537), the greatest Byzantine church. Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured the city on 29 May 1453 using giant cannons, ending the Byzantine (Eastern Roman) Empire; Hagia Sophia became a mosque. It was the Ottoman capital until 1922 (Ankara became capital of the Turkish Republic in 1923). Hagia Sophia was a museum from 1934 and reconverted to a mosque in 2020.' } ],
  ['Edirne — Selimiye',41.678,26.559,'Turkey','Ottoman capital c. 1369–1453 (Adrianople); Selimiye Mosque by Mimar Sinan (1575); UNESCO.','dyn',
   { old: 'Uskudama (Thracian) → Hadrianopolis / Adrianople (Hadrian, 2nd c.) → Edirne',
     more: 'Founded as Hadrianopolis by the Roman emperor Hadrian; site of the Roman defeat by the Goths (378). Murad I captured it c. 1369 and it became the Ottoman capital until the fall of Constantinople in 1453. The Selimiye Mosque (1568–1575), built for Selim II by the architect Mimar Sinan, who called it his masterpiece, has one of the largest domes in Islamic architecture; UNESCO 2011. Treaty of Adrianople (1829) followed the Russo-Turkish War.' } ],
  ['Bursa — Green Mosque',40.182,29.074,'Turkey','First Ottoman capital (1326, Orhan); Green Mosque; tombs of the early sultans; UNESCO.','dyn',
   { old: 'Prusa (Bithynian/Byzantine); Brusa',
     more: 'Orhan Gazi captured Byzantine Prusa in 1326, making it the first Ottoman capital (until Edirne c. 1365–69). The Green Mosque (Yeşil Cami, 1419–1421) and Green Tomb of Mehmed I are famed for green-blue Iznik-type tiles. Tombs of Osman I and Orhan lie here. Bursa was also a silk trade centre. "Bursa and Cumalıkızık: the Birth of the Ottoman Empire" was listed by UNESCO in 2014.' } ],
  ['Ankara (1402)',40.15,32.95,'Turkey','Timur defeats and captures Ottoman Sultan Bayezid I on the Çubuk plain, 1402; Ottoman interregnum follows.','battle',
   { old: 'Ancyra (Galatian/Roman); Angora',
     more: 'On 20 July 1402, Timur defeated the Ottoman sultan Bayezid I "Yildirim" on the Çubuk plain near Ankara; Bayezid was captured and died in captivity (1403). The defeat triggered the Ottoman Interregnum (1402–1413), a civil war among his sons until Mehmed I reunited the state, and delayed the fall of Constantinople by about 50 years. Timur had sacked Delhi in 1398. Ankara became capital of the Turkish Republic in 1923.' } ],
  ['Konya — Mevlana',37.871,32.505,'Turkey','Capital of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum; tomb of Jalaluddin Rumi (Mevlevi whirling dervishes).','dyn',
   { old: 'Iconium (Greco-Roman; visited by St Paul); Qunya',
     more: 'Konya was the capital of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum (c. 1097–1308), the Turkish state in Anatolia that grew after Manzikert. The Persian poet-mystic Jalaluddin Rumi (born Balkh 1207, died Konya 1273), author of the Masnavi, lived here; his followers founded the Mevlevi order of whirling dervishes (sema ceremony, UNESCO intangible heritage). His green-domed tomb is now the Mevlana Museum. Seljuk caravanserais and madrasas survive.' } ],
  ['Manzikert (1071)',39.1478,42.5442,'Turkey (Malazgirt)','Seljuk Alp Arslan defeats Byzantine Romanos IV, 1071; Anatolia opens to the Turks; prelude to the Crusades.','battle',
   { old: 'Manzikert / Mantzikert (Byzantine Armenia) → Malazgirt',
     more: 'On 26 August 1071 the Seljuk sultan Alp Arslan defeated and captured the Byzantine emperor Romanos IV Diogenes near Manzikert, north of Lake Van. Turkmen tribes then poured into Anatolia, leading to the Sultanate of Rum and the gradual Turkification of Asia Minor. The Byzantine appeal for help that followed contributed to Pope Urban II calling the First Crusade (1095). The exact battlefield is uncertain.' } ],
  ['Tabriz — Blue Mosque',38.074,46.301,'Iran','Ilkhanate capital under Ghazan; first Safavid capital of Shah Ismail I (1501); Blue Mosque (1465).','dyn',
   { old: 'Tauris (medieval European name); Tabrez',
     more: 'Tabriz was capital of the Mongol Ilkhanate under Abaqa and Ghazan Khan (who converted to Islam, 1295), and of the Turkmen Qara Qoyunlu and Aq Qoyunlu. The Blue Mosque (Kabud Mosque, 1465), famed for blue tilework, was built under Jahan Shah of the Qara Qoyunlu. Shah Ismail I crowned himself here in 1501, founding the Safavid dynasty and making Twelver Shiism the state religion. The Tabriz Historic Bazaar is a UNESCO site.' } ],
  ['Chaldiran (1514)',39.089,44.327,'Iran (West Azerbaijan)','Ottoman Selim I’s artillery & muskets defeat Safavid Ismail I, 1514; fixes the Sunni–Shia frontier.','battle',
   { old: 'Çaldıran (Turkish) / Chaldoran',
     more: 'On 23 August 1514 the Ottoman sultan Selim I defeated the Safavid Shah Ismail I at Chaldiran in north-west Iran. Ottoman Janissaries with muskets and field artillery behind a wagon barricade crushed the Safavid Qizilbash cavalry; Babur later used similar gunpowder tactics at Panipat (1526). Selim briefly occupied Tabriz; eastern Anatolia passed to the Ottomans, roughly fixing the Ottoman–Safavid (Sunni–Shia) frontier.' } ],
  ['Ardabil — Sheikh Safi Shrine',38.249,48.291,'Iran','Seat of the Safavid Sufi order; shrine of Sheikh Safi al-Din, ancestor of Shah Ismail I; UNESCO.','holy',
   { old: 'Artavil / Badan Pirouz (Sasanian-era names)',
     more: 'Ardabil was home of Sheikh Safi al-Din (1252–1334), founder of the Safaviyya Sufi order, from which the Safavid dynasty took its name. His descendant Ismail I made the order the basis of a state in 1501. The shrine complex was enlarged by Shah Abbas I, who donated Chinese porcelain; the famous Ardabil Carpet (1539–40), now in London\'s V&A Museum, came from here. UNESCO World Heritage Site 2010.' } ],
  ['Isfahan — Naqsh-e Jahan',32.658,51.678,'Iran','Safavid capital from 1598 (Shah Abbas I); Naqsh-e Jahan Square, Shah Mosque, Ali Qapu; UNESCO.','dyn',
   { old: 'Aspadana / Gabae (ancient); Sepahan; "Isfahan nesf-e jahan" ("half the world")',
     more: 'Seljuk capital under Malik Shah (Friday Mosque), Isfahan was made the Safavid capital by Shah Abbas I in 1598. He laid out Naqsh-e Jahan Square (now Imam Square) with the Shah (Imam) Mosque, the Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque, the Ali Qapu palace and the Grand Bazaar. Armenian merchants settled in New Julfa traded silk with India and Europe. Shah Abbas was a contemporary of Akbar and Jahangir and seized Kandahar from the Mughals in 1622. UNESCO 1979.' } ],
  ['Nishapur',36.206,58.793,'Iran (Khorasan)','Tahirid & early Seljuk centre in Khorasan; home of Omar Khayyam; razed by the Mongols, 1221.','dyn',
   { old: 'Nev-Shapur ("New Shapur", founded by Sasanian Shapur I, 3rd c.); Abarshahr',
     more: 'Founded by the Sasanian Shapur I and refounded by Shapur II, Nishapur was a great city of Khorasan on the Silk Road, capital of the Tahirids (821–873) and an early Seljuk centre (Tughril Beg, 1037). It was home of the poet-mathematician Omar Khayyam (Rubaiyat) and the Sufi poet Farid al-Din Attar. In 1221 the Mongols under Tolui razed it after Chinggis Khan\'s son-in-law was killed there. Famous for turquoise mines and pottery.' } ],
  ['Merv',37.663,62.193,'Turkmenistan','Oasis on the Murghab; Abbasid revolt launched here (747); Seljuk capital of Sanjar; destroyed by Mongols 1221.','dyn',
   { old: 'Margiana / Antiochia Margiana (Seleucid); Mouru (Avestan); Marw al-Shahijan',
     more: 'Merv, an oasis on the Murghab river, was a major Silk Road city: Achaemenid Margiana, Seleucid Antiochia Margiana and a Sasanian centre where Yazdegerd III was killed (651). Abu Muslim launched the Abbasid revolution from Merv in 747, and al-Mamun ruled from here (813–818). As the Seljuk capital of Sultan Sanjar it was among the world\'s largest cities, before Tolui\'s Mongols massacred its people in 1221. State Historical and Cultural Park "Ancient Merv", UNESCO 1999.' } ],
  ['Herat — Citadel',34.346,62.189,'Afghanistan','Timurid capital of Shah Rukh & Husain Baiqara; painter Bihzad, poet Jami; Timurid renaissance.','dyn',
   { old: 'Haraiva / Aria (Achaemenid); Alexandria Ariana; Hari',
     more: 'Herat was capital of the Timurid ruler Shah Rukh (r. 1405–1447), whose wife Gawhar Shad built the Musalla complex, and of Sultan Husayn Bayqara (r. 1469–1506). The court patronised the painter Bihzad, the poet Jami and Mir Ali Sher Navai (Chagatai Turkic) — the "Timurid Renaissance" which shaped early Mughal art; Babur visited in 1506. The citadel (Qala Ikhtiyaruddin) dates back to Alexander\'s era, rebuilt by the Kartids and Shah Rukh.' } ],
  ['Jam — Minaret (Firozkoh)',34.397,64.516,'Afghanistan (Ghor)','65 m Ghurid minaret (c. 1190s) of Ghiyas-ud-din, at site of the Ghurid capital Firozkoh; Muhammad Ghori’s dynasty.','dyn',
   { old: 'Firozkoh ("Turquoise Mountain"), summer capital of the Ghurids',
     more: 'The 65 m baked-brick Minaret of Jam, on the Hari river in remote Ghor, was built c. 1190s (traditionally 1194) by the Ghurid sultan Ghiyas-ud-din Muhammad, elder brother of Muhammad of Ghor (Muizz-ud-din), probably celebrating victories. It is covered with Kufic and Naskh inscriptions including Surah Maryam and is the second tallest brick minaret after Delhi\'s Qutb Minar, which it inspired. Probably marks Firozkoh. First Afghan UNESCO site (2002), on the Danger list.' } ],
  ['Ghazni',33.549,68.423,'Afghanistan','Ghaznavid capital; Mahmud raided India 17 times (1000–1027; Somnath 1025); Al-Biruni & Firdausi at court.','dyn',
   { old: 'Ghazna / Ghaznin; Alexandria in Opiana (attributed)',
     more: 'Ghazni was capital of the Ghaznavid dynasty founded by Alptigin and Sabuktigin. Mahmud of Ghazni (r. 998–1030) raided India about 17 times (1000–1027), sacking Mathura, Kannauj, Thanesar and the Somnath temple (1025–26). His court hosted Al-Biruni (Kitab-ul-Hind) and Firdausi (Shahnama). Ala-ud-din Husain of Ghor burnt Ghazni in 1150/51 ("Jahan-suz"); it then served as a Ghurid base for Muhammad Ghori\'s Indian campaigns. Minarets of Bahram Shah and Masud III survive.' } ],
  ['Kandahar',31.62,65.716,'Afghanistan','Contested by Mughals & Safavids; finally lost to Persia in 1649 under Shah Jahan despite three sieges.','dyn',
   { old: 'Alexandria Arachosia (Alexander, 4th c. BCE); Qandahar',
     more: 'Founded or refounded by Alexander as Alexandria in Arachosia, it is the site of Ashoka\'s bilingual Greek–Aramaic rock edict found at Chil Zena (1958). In the 16th–17th centuries it guarded the route from Persia to India and changed hands between the Mughals and Safavids repeatedly; Shah Abbas I took it in 1622, Shah Jahan regained it in 1638, and it was lost in 1649 — Aurangzeb (1649, 1652) and Dara Shikoh (1653) failed to retake it. Ahmad Shah Durrani made it his capital (1747).' } ],
  ['Kabul — Bagh-e Babur',34.503,69.158,'Afghanistan','Babur ruled Kabul from 1504 before invading India (Panipat 1526); buried in Bagh-e Babur.','dyn',
   { old: 'Kubha (Rigvedic name of the Kabul river); Kabura (Ptolemy)',
     more: 'Babur, a Timurid prince expelled from Fergana, seized Kabul in 1504 and ruled it for over two decades before defeating Ibrahim Lodi at the First Battle of Panipat (1526). He laid out a terraced garden (Bagh-e Babur) and asked to be buried there; his remains were moved from Agra to Kabul c. 1540s. Kabul stayed a Mughal province until Nadir Shah took it in 1738. Timur Shah Durrani moved the Afghan capital from Kandahar to Kabul in 1776.' } ],
  ['Balkh',36.758,66.898,'Afghanistan','Ancient Bactra, "mother of cities"; Rumi born here (1207); destroyed by Chinggis Khan, 1220.','dyn',
   { old: 'Bactra / Zariaspa (Greek); Bakhdi (Avestan); "Umm al-Bilad" (mother of cities)',
     more: 'Balkh was capital of ancient Bactria and of the Greco-Bactrian kingdom after Alexander. Zoroastrian tradition links it with Zoroaster. It was a major Buddhist centre (Nava Vihara, seen by Xuanzang c. 630) — the Barmakid viziers of the Abbasids descended from its Buddhist abbots. The Persian poet Rumi was born here in 1207. Chinggis Khan destroyed the city in 1220, and Timur rebuilt parts; the shrine of Khwaja Abu Nasr Parsa (15th c.) survives.' } ],
  ['Bukhara — Ark',39.778,64.41,'Uzbekistan','Samanid capital (9–10th c.); Ark citadel, Kalyan minaret; Mongol sack 1220; later Uzbek khanate; UNESCO.','dyn',
   { old: 'Bukhar (from Sanskrit vihara, "monastery"; attributed); Numijkat',
     more: 'Bukhara was capital of the Persian Samanid dynasty (819–999), which revived New Persian literature (Rudaki) and patronised the physician-philosopher Ibn Sina (Avicenna). The Samanid Mausoleum (10th c.), the Kalyan Minaret (1127, spared by Chinggis Khan) and the Ark fortress survive. Chinggis Khan sacked it in 1220. Later it was capital of the Shaybanid Uzbeks and the Emirate of Bukhara (Russian protectorate 1868). Historic Centre UNESCO 1993.' } ],
  ['Samarkand — Registan',39.655,66.976,'Uzbekistan','Timur’s capital from 1370; Gur-e-Amir tomb; Ulugh Beg observatory; Registan madrasas; UNESCO.','dyn',
   { old: 'Maracanda (Greek, taken by Alexander 329 BCE); Afrasiab (old city mound); Sogdian capital',
     more: 'Ancient Maracanda was the main city of Sogdiana, the Silk Road\'s merchant people. Arabs took it in 712; Chinggis Khan sacked it in 1220. Timur (Tamerlane) made it his capital from 1370 and built the Bibi-Khanym Mosque and Gur-e Amir mausoleum, where he lies (died 1405). His grandson Ulugh Beg built an observatory (1420s) and the first madrasa of the Registan; Sher-Dor and Tilya-Kori were added in the 17th c. Babur briefly held Samarkand three times. UNESCO 2001.' } ],
  ['Andijan (Fergana)',40.783,72.334,'Uzbekistan','Capital of the Fergana valley; Babur born here 1483 and became ruler of Fergana in 1494, aged 11.','dyn',
   { old: 'Andukan / Andigan; Fergana valley = Dayuan (Han Chinese "Ta-yuan")',
     more: 'Andijan was the capital of Fergana, the fertile valley famed in Han China (Dayuan) for "heavenly horses". Zahir-ud-din Muhammad Babur was born here on 14 February 1483, a descendant of Timur on his father\'s side and of Chinggis Khan on his mother\'s. He inherited Fergana in 1494 aged 11. His memoir Baburnama (in Chagatai Turkic) describes Fergana lovingly; Babur Park and a museum stand in Andijan today.' } ],
  ['Talas (751)',42.525,72.233,'Kyrgyzstan–Kazakhstan','Abbasids defeat Tang China, 751 (exact site c.); ends Chinese push west; papermaking said to spread west after.','battle',
   { old: 'Taraz / Talas (Kazakhstan); Atlakh (battle site in Arab sources)',
     more: 'In July 751 an Abbasid army under Ziyad ibn Salih, with Karluk Turks who switched sides, defeated the Tang Chinese army of the Korean-born general Gao Xianzhi near the Talas river. Tang expansion into Central Asia halted, especially after the An Lushan rebellion (755). Tradition holds that Chinese prisoners taught papermaking in Samarkand, from where it spread to Baghdad and Europe, though paper may have arrived earlier. Exact battlefield uncertain.' } ]
 ]
});

/* ======================= MONGOLS & TIMURIDS ======================= */
GEO.add({
 id:'his-mongols', group:'his-world', title:'Mongols, Timurids & Central Asian Powers', bn:'মঙ্গোল, তৈমুরীয় ও মধ্য এশিয়ার শক্তি', icon:'🏹', world:true,
 intro:'চেঙ্গিস খানের অভিযান (১২১৯ ওত্রার) থেকে চার খানাত — ইউয়ান, ইলখানাত, চাগতাই, গোল্ডেন হোর্ড — রাজধানী সহ মনে রাখো; বাগদাদ (১২৫৮), আইন জালুত (১২৬০) আর তৈমুরের রাজধানী সমরখন্দ ইসলামি পাতায় আছে।',
 types:{cap:['Mongol / Timurid capital','#6a1b9a'], battle:['Campaign / battle','#c62828'], sack:['City sacked / destroyed','#ef6c00']},
 pts:[
  ['Karakorum',47.21,102.848,'Mongolia (Övörkhangai)','Mongol imperial capital founded c. 1220 under Chinggis, walled by Ögedei (1235); Erdene Zuu monastery built from ruins.','cap',
   { old: 'Qaraqorum / Kharkhorin (modern town); Har Horin',
     more: 'Chinggis Khan chose the Orkhon valley as a base c. 1220; his son Ögedei built walls and a palace in 1235, making Karakorum the capital of the united Mongol Empire. Envoys such as William of Rubruck (1254) described its silver fountain-tree and its many mosques, churches and temples. Kublai moved the capital to Khanbaliq (1260s); Ming troops destroyed the city in 1388. The Erdene Zuu monastery (1585) was built with its stones. Orkhon Valley UNESCO 2004.' } ],
  ['Khanbaliq (Dadu)',39.9333,116.4,'China (Beijing)','Kublai Khan’s Yuan capital (1267–1368), Marco Polo’s "Cambaluc"; earlier Jin capital Zhongdu sacked by Chinggis 1215.','cap',
   { old: 'Ji / Yanjing → Zhongdu (Jin) → Dadu / Khanbaliq "city of the Khan" (Yuan) → Beiping → Beijing (Ming 1421)',
     more: 'After Chinggis Khan sacked the Jin capital Zhongdu in 1215, Kublai Khan built a new planned capital, Dadu (Turkic Khanbaliq), from 1267 and proclaimed the Yuan dynasty in 1271. Marco Polo described it as "Cambaluc". The Grand Canal was extended to supply it. The Ming took it in 1368 and renamed it Beiping; Yongle rebuilt it as Beijing with the Forbidden City (capital from 1421).' } ],
  ['Shangdu (Xanadu)',42.358,116.185,'China (Inner Mongolia)','Kublai Khan’s summer capital (1256); Coleridge’s "Xanadu"; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Kaiping (1256) → Shangdu ("Upper Capital", 1263); Xanadu (European)',
     more: 'Kublai Khan founded Kaiping in 1256 as his base; he was proclaimed Great Khan here in 1260, and it was renamed Shangdu, the Yuan summer capital, in 1263. Marco Polo described its marble palace and park; Coleridge\'s poem "Kubla Khan" (1797) made "Xanadu" a byword for splendour. It was a meeting point of Mongol nomadic and Chinese urban traditions; the Ming burnt it in 1369. UNESCO World Heritage Site 2012.' } ],
  ['Zhongxing (Yinchuan)',38.485,106.225,'China (Ningxia)','Western Xia (Tangut) capital; Chinggis Khan died during this campaign, 1227; the Tangut state was annihilated.','sack',
   { old: 'Xingqing / Zhongxing-fu (Western Xia capital); Ningxia (Yuan–Qing)',
     more: 'Zhongxing was the capital of the Western Xia (Xi Xia), a Buddhist state of the Tangut people (1038–1227) with its own script. Chinggis Khan campaigned against it repeatedly from 1205; during the final siege he died in August 1227 (cause uncertain). The Mongols then massacred the population and destroyed the Tangut state. The Western Xia imperial tombs survive west of Yinchuan, capital of today\'s Ningxia Hui region.' } ],
  ['Kaifeng (1233)',34.795,114.345,'China (Henan)','Jin dynasty’s last capital; fell to Ögedei’s Mongols in 1233 after a long siege; Jin ended 1234.','sack',
   { old: 'Daliang (Wei, Warring States); Bianjing / Dongjing (Northern Song); Nanjing / Bianliang (Jin)',
     more: 'Kaifeng was the capital of the Northern Song (960–1127), when it was one of the world\'s largest cities, depicted in the scroll "Along the River During the Qingming Festival". The Jurchen Jin took it in 1127 and later made it their capital after fleeing Zhongdu. Ögedei\'s general Subutai besieged it in 1232–33, when gunpowder bombs ("thunder-crash bombs") were used; it fell in 1233 and the Jin ended in 1234. A small Jewish community lived here.' } ],
  ['Xiangyang (1268–73)',32.01,112.122,'China (Hubei)','Long Mongol siege (1268–73) of the Song twin fortress; Persian counterweight trebuchets used; opened the Yangtze to Kublai.','battle',
   { old: 'Xiangyang & Fancheng twin cities → merged as Xiangfan (1950) → Xiangyang (2010)',
     more: 'The twin fortress cities of Xiangyang and Fancheng on the Han river guarded the route into the Southern Song heartland. Kublai Khan\'s forces besieged them for about five years (1268–1273). The siege was broken after two Muslim engineers from the Ilkhanate, Ismail and Ala al-Din, built counterweight trebuchets ("Muslim catapults"). The fall of Xiangyang opened the Yangtze valley and led to the conquest of the Song (1276–79).' } ],
  ['Hangzhou (Lin’an)',30.247,120.144,'China (Zhejiang)','Southern Song capital; surrendered to Kublai’s Yuan in 1276; Marco Polo’s "Quinsai"; Ibn Battuta visited.','battle',
   { old: 'Lin\'an ("temporary peace", Southern Song); Qiantang; Quinsai / Kinsay (Marco Polo)',
     more: 'After the Jin captured Kaifeng (1127), the Song court fled south and made Lin\'an (Hangzhou) its capital in 1138. It became perhaps the world\'s largest and richest city, at the southern end of the Grand Canal by the West Lake. Bayan\'s Yuan army took it in 1276; the last Song loyalists died at the naval battle of Yamen (1279). Marco Polo called it "Quinsai", the finest city in the world; Ibn Battuta visited c. 1345. West Lake is UNESCO-listed (2011).' } ],
  ['Hakata Bay (1274, 1281)',33.618,130.333,'Japan (Fukuoka)','Kublai’s two invasions of Japan repulsed; storms called kamikaze ("divine wind"); Genkō Bōrui defence wall.','battle',
   { old: 'Hakata (port, now part of Fukuoka); Genkō (Japanese name of the invasions)',
     more: 'Kublai Khan sent Mongol, Korean (Goryeo) and Chinese fleets against Kamakura-shogunate Japan in 1274 (Bun\'ei) and 1281 (Kōan). Samurai resisted at Hakata, helped by a stone defence wall (Genkō Bōrui) built after the first attack. On both occasions storms or typhoons wrecked much of the fleet; the Japanese called them kamikaze ("divine wind"). The term was later used for WWII suicide pilots.' } ],
  ['Bạch Đằng (1288)',20.937,106.629,'Vietnam (Quảng Ninh)','Trần Hưng Đạo destroys the Mongol-Yuan fleet using stakes planted in the river bed, 1288.','battle',
   { old: 'Bạch Đằng Giang ("White Wave river"), also site of battles in 938 and 981',
     more: 'In April 1288 the Vietnamese commander Trần Hưng Đạo of the Trần dynasty planted iron-tipped wooden stakes in the bed of the Bạch Đằng river estuary. At high tide he lured the Yuan fleet of Omar (Ô Mã Nhi) in, and the falling tide impaled and trapped it. It ended Kublai\'s third invasion of Đại Việt; Ngô Quyền had used the same tactic against the Southern Han in 938. A national symbol of Vietnamese resistance.' } ],
  ['Almaliq',44.24,80.533,'China (Xinjiang, Ili valley)','Capital of the Chagatai Khanate (13–14th c.) in the Ili valley near Huocheng.','cap',
   { old: 'Almalik / Almalyk ("apple place"); Alimali (Chinese); near modern Huocheng',
     more: 'Almaliq in the Ili valley was a Karluk town that became the main seat of the Chagatai Khanate, ruled by descendants of Chinggis Khan\'s second son Chagatai, in the 13th–14th centuries. It lay on the northern Silk Road and hosted Christian (Nestorian and Franciscan) missions; a Catholic bishop was martyred here c. 1339. Tughlugh Timur, khan of Moghulistan who converted to Islam, is buried in a tiled mausoleum (1363) at Huocheng.' } ],
  ['Otrar (1219)',42.853,68.303,'Kazakhstan','Khwarezmian governor massacred a Mongol caravan (1218); Chinggis besieged Otrar 1219–20, starting the western campaign.','sack',
   { old: 'Farab (birthplace of al-Farabi); Tarband / Turarband',
     more: 'Otrar on the Syr Darya was a frontier town of the Khwarezmian Empire. In 1218 its governor Inalchuq massacred a Mongol-sponsored trade caravan, and Shah Ala al-Din Muhammad then killed Chinggis Khan\'s envoys. In revenge Chinggis invaded; Otrar fell after a siege of about five months (1219–20) and Inalchuq was executed. Timur died at Otrar in February 1405 while marching on Ming China. The philosopher al-Farabi was from the region.' } ],
  ['Kunya-Urgench (Gurganj)',42.326,59.152,'Turkmenistan','Capital of the Khwarezm Shahs; Mongols destroyed it and flooded it with the Amu Darya, 1221; UNESCO.','sack',
   { old: 'Gurganj / Jurjaniya (Khwarezmian capital); Urganch',
     more: 'Gurganj was the capital of the Khwarezm-Shah dynasty, whose empire stretched from the Aral Sea to Iran. Mongol forces under Jochi, Chagatai and Ögedei captured it in 1221 after a long siege, massacred its people and broke the dams so that the Amu Darya flooded the city. It was rebuilt under the Golden Horde and later destroyed again by Timur (1388). Survivors include the 60 m Kutlug Timur minaret and the Turabeg Khanum mausoleum. UNESCO 2005.' } ],
  ['Shahrisabz (Kesh)',39.061,66.829,'Uzbekistan','Birthplace of Timur (1336); ruins of his Ak-Saray palace; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Kesh / Kish (Sogdian); Shahrisabz = "Green City"',
     more: 'Timur (Tamerlane) was born near Kesh in 1336 into the Turkicised Mongol Barlas tribe. He built the Ak-Saray ("White Palace", begun 1380), whose shattered entrance arch was some 50 m high, and the Dorus Saodat complex for his family; his own crypt here stayed unused as he was buried in Samarkand. Ulugh Beg added the Kok Gumbaz mosque (1435). The Historic Centre of Shakhrisyabz is a UNESCO site (2000), now on the Danger list.' } ],
  ['Alamut',36.445,50.586,'Iran (Qazvin)','Nizari Ismaili ("Assassins") fortress of Hasan-i Sabbah; surrendered to Hulagu, 1256.','sack',
   { old: 'Aluh Amut ("eagle\'s nest", traditional etymology)',
     more: 'In 1090 Hasan-i Sabbah seized the mountain castle of Alamut in the Alborz range and made it the headquarters of the Nizari Ismaili state, whose fidai agents were called "Assassins" (hashishiyyin) by enemies. The scholar Nasir al-Din Tusi worked here. In 1256 the last imam Rukn al-Din Khurshah surrendered to Hulagu, who destroyed the castle and its library. The Aga Khans descend from the Nizari imams and moved to India in the 1840s.' } ],
  ['Maragheh Observatory',37.396,46.209,'Iran (East Azerbaijan)','Hulagu’s first Ilkhanate capital; observatory of Nasir al-Din Tusi (1259).','cap',
   { old: 'Afrazah-Rud (old name of Maragheh); Rasadkhaneh-ye Maragheh',
     more: 'Hulagu Khan, founder of the Ilkhanate, made Maragheh his first capital. In 1259 he ordered Nasir al-Din Tusi to build an observatory, which had a large library and staff of astronomers from as far as China; it produced the Zij-i Ilkhani astronomical tables (1272). The "Tusi couple" model later appeared in Copernicus\'s work. Only foundations survive on a hill west of the town.' } ],
  ['Soltaniyeh',36.434,48.794,'Iran (Zanjan)','Ilkhanate capital built by Öljeitü (1302–12); his great domed mausoleum; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Qongqur-öleng (Mongol summer pasture); Sultaniyya',
     more: 'The Ilkhan Öljeitü (r. 1304–1316) built Soltaniyeh as his capital and erected his own mausoleum (1302–1312), whose 50 m double-shell dome is among the largest brick domes in the world and influenced later Timurid and Mughal tombs such as the Taj Mahal. Öljeitü converted to Shia Islam. The town declined after Timur\'s campaigns. UNESCO World Heritage Site 2005.' } ],
  ['Aleppo (1260)',36.199,37.163,'Syria','Sacked by Hulagu’s Mongols in Jan 1260, before Ain Jalut; sacked again by Timur in 1400.','sack',
   { old: 'Halab (Arabic); Beroea (Seleucid–Byzantine); Halpa / Halab (Bronze Age)',
     more: 'One of the oldest continuously inhabited cities, Aleppo was a terminus of caravan routes to the Mediterranean. Hulagu\'s Mongols, with Armenian and Frankish allies, took the city in January 1260 after a short siege and massacred many inhabitants; the citadel fell soon after. The Mamluks regained it after Ain Jalut. Timur sacked Aleppo again in 1400. The citadel and souqs, a UNESCO site, were heavily damaged in the Syrian civil war (2012–16).' } ],
  ['Kalka River (1223)',47.251,37.496,'Ukraine (Donetsk)','Subutai & Jebe rout the Rus’ princes and Cumans, 1223 — first Mongol clash with the Rus’.','battle',
   { old: 'Kalka (modern Kalchyk river, near the Sea of Azov)',
     more: 'During a great reconnaissance raid west of the Caspian after the fall of Khwarezm, Subutai and Jebe faked a retreat and destroyed a larger allied army of Rus\' princes (Kyiv, Galicia, Chernigov) and Cumans (Polovtsians) on 31 May 1223. Captured princes were crushed under wooden boards on which the Mongols feasted. The Mongols then withdrew, returning under Batu in 1237. Exact location disputed.' } ],
  ['Vladimir (1238)',56.127,40.397,'Russia','Batu’s Mongols sack Vladimir, capital of north-east Rus’, Feb 1238; the Golden Gate survives.','sack',
   { old: 'Vladimir-on-Klyazma (Vladimir-Zalessky); capital of Vladimir-Suzdal principality',
     more: 'Founded c. 1108 by Vladimir Monomakh, Vladimir became the capital of the Grand Principality of Vladimir-Suzdal under Andrei Bogolyubsky. Batu Khan\'s Mongol army stormed it on 7–8 February 1238; the grand prince\'s family died in the burning Assumption Cathedral. The white-stone Golden Gate (1164), Assumption Cathedral and Cathedral of St Demetrius survive and form part of a UNESCO site (1992).' } ],
  ['Kyiv (1240)',50.45,30.523,'Ukraine','Sacked by Batu Khan, Dec 1240; end of Kievan Rus’; Rus’ lands under the "Tatar yoke".','sack',
   { old: 'Kiev (Russian-derived spelling) → Kyiv; Kuyaba (Arabic); Sambatas (Byzantine)',
     more: 'Kyiv was capital of Kievan Rus\', where Prince Vladimir adopted Orthodox Christianity in 988. Batu Khan and Subutai stormed it on 6 December 1240 after a siege; the Church of the Tithes collapsed on its defenders. The fall marked the end of Kievan Rus\' as a power and began some two centuries of Golden Horde overlordship (the "Tatar yoke"). St Sophia Cathedral (11th c.) and the Kyiv-Pechersk Lavra are UNESCO-listed.' } ],
  ['Legnica / Liegnitz (1241)',51.145,16.223,'Poland','Mongols defeat Polish knights & military orders under Henry II the Pious, Apr 1241.','battle',
   { old: 'Liegnitz (German); Legnickie Pole = Wahlstatt (battlefield village)',
     more: 'On 9 April 1241 a Mongol diversionary force under Baidar and Kadan defeated the army of Duke Henry II the Pious of Silesia, including Teutonic and Templar knights, at Legnickie Pole near Legnica; Henry was killed and his head displayed on a spear. Two days later the main Mongol army won at Mohi in Hungary. The Mongols withdrew from Europe in 1242, probably after Ögedei\'s death.' } ],
  ['Mohi (1241)',47.978,20.913,'Hungary','Batu & Subutai crush King Béla IV of Hungary on the Sajó river, Apr 1241.','battle',
   { old: 'Muhi (Hungarian village); battle on the Sajó river',
     more: 'On 11 April 1241 Batu and Subutai surrounded and destroyed the army of King Béla IV of Hungary near Mohi on the Sajó river, using a night river crossing and catapults. Béla fled to the Adriatic coast and Hungary was devastated. The Mongols withdrew in 1242, probably because of the death of Great Khan Ögedei and the election (kurultai) that followed. Béla then rebuilt Hungary with stone castles.' } ],
  ['Sarai Batu',47.169,47.452,'Russia (Astrakhan, Selitrennoye)','Golden Horde capital on the Akhtuba founded by Batu (c. 1250s; site disputed); capital later moved to New Sarai.','cap',
   { old: 'Sarai ("palace"), Old Sarai; Sarai al-Mahrusa; later capital New Sarai (Sarai Berke)',
     more: 'Batu Khan, grandson of Chinggis Khan and founder of the Golden Horde (Ulus of Jochi), founded Sarai on the Akhtuba branch of the lower Volga c. 1250s. It became a large trading city with mosques and Orthodox and Catholic churches; Ibn Battuta visited the Golden Horde in 1332–33. The capital later shifted to New Sarai (Sarai Berke), and Timur destroyed the cities c. 1395. A reconstructed "Sarai Batu" film set stands near Selitrennoye.' } ],
  ['Kulikovo (1380)',53.653,38.654,'Russia (Tula)','Dmitry Donskoy of Moscow defeats the Golden Horde’s Mamai, 1380 — first major Rus’ victory over the Tatars.','battle',
   { old: 'Kulikovo Pole ("Snipe Field") at the Nepryadva–Don confluence',
     more: 'On 8 September 1380 Grand Prince Dmitry Ivanovich of Moscow defeated the Golden Horde army of the emir Mamai on the Kulikovo field near the Don, earning the name "Donskoy". Blessed by St Sergius of Radonezh, the victory raised Moscow\'s prestige as leader of the Rus\' lands, though Tokhtamysh burned Moscow in 1382. Tatar overlordship formally ended with the "Great Stand on the Ugra River" (1480) under Ivan III.' } ]
 ]
});

/* ======================= SILK ROAD & TRADE ROUTES ======================= */
GEO.add({
 id:'his-silk', group:'his-world', title:'Silk Road & Ancient Trade Routes', bn:'রেশম পথ ও প্রাচীন বাণিজ্যপথ', icon:'🐫', world:true,
 intro:'স্থলপথ: চাংআন → দুনহুয়াং → তারিম অববাহিকা (কাশগর) → পামির → মার্ভ/বুখারা → মেসোপটেমিয়া → ভূমধ্যসাগর। সমুদ্রপথ: লোহিত সাগরের বেরেনিকে থেকে মৌসুমি বায়ুতে মুজিরিস — কোথায় কী জিনিস, ভারতের যোগ কী, সেটাই প্রশ্ন।',
 types:{land:['Overland Silk Road','#8d6e63'], port:['Maritime route port','#0277bd'], centre:['Trade centre / emporium','#2e7d32']},
 pts:[
  ['Chang’an (Xi’an)',34.261,108.942,'China (Shaanxi)','Han & Tang capital; eastern start of the Silk Road (Zhang Qian’s missions, 2nd c. BCE); silk, paper went west.','land',
   { old: 'Chang\'an ("perpetual peace", Han–Tang) → Fengyuan (Ming) → Xi\'an ("western peace", 1369); Hao / Xianyang nearby',
     more: 'Chang\'an was the capital of the Western Han and the Tang (618–907), when it had about a million people and was the world\'s largest city. Emperor Wu of Han sent Zhang Qian to Central Asia (138 and 119 BCE), opening the Silk Road. Xuanzang set out from here (629) and returned with Indian texts. The Terracotta Army of Qin Shi Huang lies nearby. The "Chang\'an–Tianshan corridor" Silk Road network is UNESCO-listed (2014).' } ],
  ['Lanzhou',36.061,103.827,'China (Gansu)','Yellow River crossing at the head of the Hexi (Gansu) Corridor on the Silk Road.','land',
   { old: 'Jincheng ("Golden City", Han); Gaolan',
     more: 'Lanzhou grew at a crossing of the Yellow River at the eastern head of the Hexi (Gansu) Corridor, the narrow strip between the Qilian mountains and the Gobi desert through which the Silk Road ran towards Dunhuang. Han emperor Wu established commanderies here after expelling the Xiongnu (2nd c. BCE). It is now capital of Gansu province; the Bingling Temple caves (5th c.) are nearby.' } ],
  ['Dunhuang — Mogao Caves',40.037,94.804,'China (Gansu)','Oasis where the Silk Road forks; Mogao: ~490 Buddhist cave temples (4–14th c.); Diamond Sutra (868), oldest dated printed book.','centre',
   { old: 'Shazhou ("Sand Prefecture"); Dunhuang ("blazing beacon"); Qianfodong ("Caves of the Thousand Buddhas")',
     more: 'Dunhuang is the oasis where the Silk Road split into the northern and southern routes around the Taklamakan. The Mogao Caves, begun c. 366 CE, contain about 492 decorated cave temples with murals and sculptures from the 4th to 14th centuries, showing Indian, Central Asian and Chinese styles. The sealed "Library Cave" (Cave 17), found in 1900, yielded the Diamond Sutra (868), the oldest dated printed book; Aurel Stein took much of it in 1907. UNESCO 1987.' } ],
  ['Yumen Pass (Jade Gate)',40.354,93.864,'China (Gansu)','Han frontier gate west of Dunhuang, named after the Khotan jade that entered China through it.','land',
   { old: 'Yumenguan ("Jade Gate Pass"); Xiaofangpan site',
     more: 'The Yumen Pass, with its twin the Yang Pass, was the Han dynasty\'s western gateway at the end of the Great Wall extension into the Gansu corridor (c. 2nd–1st c. BCE). Jade from Khotan entered China through it, hence the name. Beyond lay the "Western Regions" (Xiyu). The mud-brick fort ruins are part of the UNESCO Silk Roads site (2014) and were celebrated in Tang frontier poetry.' } ],
  ['Turfan — Gaochang',42.853,89.529,'China (Xinjiang)','Turpan oasis city on the northern route; Xuanzang stayed here (c. 630); Uyghur kingdom; Buddhist & Manichaean texts.','land',
   { old: 'Qocho / Khocho (Uyghur); Karakhoja; Gaochang (Chinese)',
     more: 'Gaochang in the Turpan depression was a key northern-route oasis. The Buddhist pilgrim Xuanzang stayed here c. 630 as guest of King Qu Wentai, who sponsored his onward journey to India. From the 9th century it was a capital of the Uyghur (Qocho) kingdom, where Manichaeism, Buddhism and Nestorian Christianity flourished; many manuscripts and murals were taken by German expeditions (Le Coq, 1900s). Part of the UNESCO Silk Roads site (2014).' } ],
  ['Kashgar',39.472,75.984,'China (Xinjiang)','Junction where the northern & southern Tarim routes meet before crossing the Pamirs to Fergana and India.','centre',
   { old: 'Shule (Han Chinese); Kashi (modern Chinese)',
     more: 'Kashgar, at the western edge of the Tarim basin, is where the northern and southern Silk Road routes met before crossing the Pamirs into Fergana, Bactria and India. It was a Buddhist kingdom visited by Xuanzang and later capital of the Turkic Kara-Khanids, the first Turkic dynasty to adopt Islam (10th c.). Kushan influence reached it in the 1st–2nd c. CE. In the 19th century it was a centre of the Great Game, with British and Russian consulates.' } ],
  ['Yarkand',38.391,77.223,'China (Xinjiang)','Southern Tarim oasis; caravan route over the Karakoram Pass to Leh (Ladakh) and Kashmir.','land',
   { old: 'Shache (Han Chinese "Sha-ch\'e"); Yarkant',
     more: 'Yarkand was an oasis kingdom on the southern Tarim route and later the capital of the Yarkent Khanate (1514–1705) founded by the Chagatayid Sultan Said Khan, a relative of Babur. It was the main starting point for caravans over the Karakoram Pass to Leh in Ladakh and on to Kashmir and Punjab, trading pashmina, charas, silk and horses until the 1940s. Mirza Haidar Dughlat, author of the Tarikh-i-Rashidi and conqueror of Kashmir (1540), served its khans.' } ],
  ['Khotan',37.117,79.917,'China (Xinjiang)','Jade oasis on the southern Tarim route; Buddhist kingdom using Kharosthi script & Gandhari Prakrit documents.','land',
   { old: 'Gostana / Kustana (Prakrit / Sanskrit); Yutian (Chinese); Hotan',
     more: 'Khotan, on the southern Tarim route, was famed for jade from the Kunlun rivers. According to tradition, it was founded by a son of Ashoka (Kunala legend), and it became a major centre of Mahayana Buddhism; Faxian (401) saw its grand processions. Administrative documents in Gandhari Prakrit in Kharosthi script and Khotanese Saka texts have been found. Legend says silkworm eggs were smuggled into Khotan in a princess\'s headdress. Conquered by the Kara-Khanids c. 1006.' } ],
  ['Tashkurgan (Stone Tower)',37.773,75.224,'China (Xinjiang)','Pamir caravan halt, often identified with Ptolemy’s "Stone Tower"; route south to Gandhara.','land',
   { old: 'Tashkurgan = "Stone Fort"; Qiepantuo (Kabhanda, Xuanzang); Ptolemy\'s Lithinos Pyrgos ("Stone Tower", attributed)',
     more: 'Tashkurgan, capital of the small Sarikoli kingdom of Kabhanda (Qiepantuo) seen by Xuanzang on his return journey (c. 649), lies in the Pamirs near the Khunjerab Pass, today the Karakoram Highway border with Pakistan. Many scholars identify it with the "Stone Tower" of Ptolemy, the meeting place of Roman-world and Chinese merchants, though other locations are proposed. The mud-brick Stone City fort survives.' } ],
  ['Ai-Khanoum',37.165,69.409,'Afghanistan (Takhar)','Greco-Bactrian city on the Oxus (Amu Darya): gymnasium, theatre, Delphic maxims; route to India.','centre',
   { old: 'Alexandria on the Oxus (identification attributed); Eucratideia (proposed)',
     more: 'Ai-Khanoum ("Lady Moon" in Uzbek) at the junction of the Oxus and Kokcha rivers was a Greco-Bactrian city founded c. 280 BCE, excavated by the French (1964–78). It had a Greek theatre, gymnasium, Corinthian columns and an inscription copying the Delphic maxims brought by Clearchus. Indian punch-marked coins and Agathocles\' coins with Indian deities (Balarama, Vasudeva-Krishna) were found. Abandoned c. 145 BCE after nomad invasions; looted during the Afghan wars.' } ],
  ['Begram (Kapisa)',34.94,69.255,'Afghanistan (Parwan)','Kushan summer capital; Begram hoard: Indian ivories, Roman glass, Chinese lacquer together.','centre',
   { old: 'Kapisi / Kapisa (Panini, Xuanzang); Alexandria in the Caucasus (attributed)',
     more: 'Kapisi was the summer capital of the Kushan emperors (1st–3rd c. CE) north of Kabul; Panini mentions Kapisi\'s wine. French excavations (1937–39) found the "Begram hoard" in sealed rooms: Indian ivory carvings (in Mathura–Sanchi style), Roman glass and bronzes, Greco-Roman plaster casts and Chinese Han lacquer, showing the Kushan role in long-distance trade. Xuanzang visited Kapisa in 630. Bagram airbase lies close by.' } ],
  ['Ctesiphon',33.094,44.581,'Iraq','Parthian & Sasanian capital on the Tigris; Taq Kasra arch; Persian middlemen in the Rome–China silk trade.','centre',
   { old: 'Tisfun (Persian); with Seleucia formed Al-Mada\'in ("the cities", Arabic); Salman Pak (modern)',
     more: 'Founded across the Tigris from Seleucia, Ctesiphon became the winter capital of the Parthian (Arsacid) and then Sasanian empires. The Taq Kasra, a vast brick barrel-vaulted iwan (c. 6th c., attributed to Khosrow I Anushirvan), is the largest single-span brick vault of antiquity. Persians controlled the land silk trade between China, India and Rome. Arabs took the city after Qadisiyyah (637), capturing huge treasure; Baghdad later replaced it.' } ],
  ['Palmyra',34.551,38.268,'Syria','Desert caravan city linking the Gulf and Euphrates to the Mediterranean; Queen Zenobia (3rd c.); UNESCO.','land',
   { old: 'Tadmor / Tadmur (Semitic, Mari tablets c. 2nd millennium BCE); "City of Palms"',
     more: 'Palmyra, an oasis in the Syrian desert, grew rich in the 1st–3rd c. CE by organising caravans between the Euphrates, the Persian Gulf (and thus India) and the Roman Mediterranean. Its Aramaic-speaking merchants built the Temple of Bel and a colonnaded street. Queen Zenobia rebelled against Rome and conquered Egypt before Emperor Aurelian captured her (272–273). ISIS destroyed several monuments in 2015–17. UNESCO 1980.' } ],
  ['Antioch',36.203,36.161,'Turkey (Antakya)','Seleucid capital; western terminus of overland routes reaching the Mediterranean.','centre',
   { old: 'Antiochia on the Orontes (Seleucid, 300 BCE) → Antakya; Theoupolis (Byzantine)',
     more: 'Seleucus I Nicator founded Antioch on the Orontes in 300 BCE, naming it after his father; it became the Seleucid capital and later the third city of the Roman Empire. Followers of Jesus were first called "Christians" here (Acts 11). It was the western terminus of overland caravan routes from Mesopotamia. The First Crusade captured it in 1098, creating the Principality of Antioch; the Mamluk Baybars destroyed it in 1268. Hit by the 2023 Turkey earthquake.' } ],
  ['Tyre',33.271,35.196,'Lebanon','Phoenician port famed for Tyrian purple dye; great Mediterranean trading city.','port',
   { old: 'Sur / Tsor ("rock", Phoenician); Tyros (Greek)',
     more: 'Tyre was the leading Phoenician city-state, founder of Carthage (traditionally 814 BCE) and famed for the costly Tyrian purple dye from murex shells, worn by royalty. Its island fortress resisted Nebuchadnezzar for 13 years; Alexander took it in 332 BCE after building a causeway, which turned the island into a peninsula. The Phoenicians spread the alphabet across the Mediterranean. Roman hippodrome and necropolis survive; UNESCO 1984.' } ],
  ['Petra — Al-Khazneh',30.322,35.452,'Jordan','Nabataean rock-cut caravan city on the incense & spice route from Arabia; UNESCO.','land',
   { old: 'Raqmu (Nabataean name); Sela (biblical, attributed); Petra = "rock" (Greek)',
     more: 'Petra was the capital of the Nabataean Arabs, who controlled the incense route carrying frankincense and myrrh from south Arabia and spices from India to Gaza and Damascus. Their rock-cut tombs and temples include Al-Khazneh ("the Treasury", c. 1st c. CE). Water was managed with dams and cisterns. Rome annexed the kingdom in 106 CE (Provincia Arabia). Rediscovered for Europe by J. L. Burckhardt in 1812. UNESCO 1985; one of the New Seven Wonders (2007).' } ],
  ['Alexandria',31.2,29.918,'Egypt','Ptolemaic & Roman emporium; Indian pepper, spices, cotton reached Rome via the Red Sea & Nile; Pharos lighthouse.','centre',
   { old: 'Rhakotis (Egyptian village on the site); Alexandria (founded by Alexander 331 BCE); Al-Iskandariya',
     more: 'Founded by Alexander the Great in 331 BCE, Alexandria was the Ptolemaic capital, home of the Library and Museum (Euclid, Eratosthenes) and of the Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders. Under Rome it was the main emporium for the India trade: goods from Muziris and Barygaza came via Red Sea ports and the Nile, as described in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea (1st c. CE). Ptolemaic rulers sent Greek envoys; Ashoka\'s edicts mention a king "Turamaya" (Ptolemy II).' } ],
  ['Ostia (port of Rome)',41.756,12.292,'Italy','Port of ancient Rome; Pliny lamented the drain of Roman gold to India for pepper, pearls & muslin.','port',
   { old: 'Ostia ("river mouth"); Portus (new harbour of Claudius & Trajan nearby)',
     more: 'Ostia at the mouth of the Tiber was Rome\'s port city; Claudius and Trajan built the larger harbour of Portus nearby. Eastern goods including Indian pepper, pearls, ivory, muslin and spices arrived here via Alexandria. Pliny the Elder (Natural History, 1st c. CE) complained that India, China and Arabia drained some 100 million sesterces a year from Rome; Roman gold coins found in South India (e.g. Arikamedu region, Coimbatore hoards) confirm the trade. Well-preserved ruins of Ostia Antica.' } ],
  ['Myos Hormos',26.157,34.242,'Egypt (Quseir al-Qadim)','Roman Red Sea port named in the Periplus; ships sailed with the monsoon to Muziris & Barygaza.','port',
   { old: 'Myos Hormos ("Mussel Harbour"); Quseir al-Qadim (Old Quseir)',
     more: 'Myos Hormos was a Red Sea port of Ptolemaic and Roman Egypt linked by a desert road to Coptos on the Nile. The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea and Strabo describe ships leaving here and Berenice each July to sail with the south-west monsoon (associated with Hippalus) to Muziris and Barygaza, returning in winter. Excavations at Quseir al-Qadim found Indian pottery, teak and an ostracon in Tamil-Brahmi. Strabo says some 120 ships a year sailed to India.' } ],
  ['Berenice',23.909,35.473,'Egypt','Roman Red Sea port; Indian black pepper, teak and Tamil-Brahmi inscribed pottery excavated.','port',
   { old: 'Berenice Troglodytica (Ptolemy II, named after his mother Berenice I); Medinet el-Haras',
     more: 'Founded c. 275 BCE by Ptolemy II for importing African war elephants, Berenice became a leading Roman port for the India trade (1st c. BCE–6th c. CE). Excavations found about 7.5 kg of Indian black pepper in a temple jar, teak wood from India, Indian fine wares, a Buddha statue and pottery with Tamil-Brahmi inscriptions naming South Indian traders, plus a Sanskrit inscription. It shows direct Tamil participation in the Red Sea trade described in the Periplus and Sangam poems.' } ],
  ['Aden',12.8,45.033,'Yemen','"Eudaemon Arabia" of the Periplus; entrepôt between India and the Red Sea for centuries.','port',
   { old: 'Eudaemon Arabia ("Arabia Felix" port, Periplus); Adana (Roman); Aden',
     more: 'Aden, a natural harbour in an extinct volcanic crater, was "Eudaemon Arabia" in the 1st-c. Periplus, a transshipment point between Indian and Egyptian ships. In medieval times it was the great entrepôt of the India–Red Sea trade (Geniza letters of Jewish merchants). The British East India Company took it in 1839 and governed it from Bombay as part of British India until 1937; it was then a Crown colony until South Yemen\'s independence (1967). A coaling station on the Suez route.' } ],
  ['Socotra — Hoq Cave',12.586,54.354,'Yemen','Island on the India–Red Sea route; Hoq Cave holds 1st–5th c. graffiti, many in Indian Brahmi, left by sailors.','port',
   { old: 'Dioscorida / Dioskouridou (Periplus); Sukhadhara ("island of bliss", Sanskrit, attributed)',
     more: 'Socotra lies on the sea route between India and the Gulf of Aden; the Periplus says it had Indian, Arab and Greek settlers. In 2000–01 Belgian explorers found over 200 inscriptions and drawings in the Hoq Cave left by visiting sailors from the 1st c. BCE to 6th c. CE, most in Indian Brahmi script (many by Gujarati/Western Indian sailors), with others in South Arabian, Ethiopic, Greek, Palmyrene and Bactrian. Endemic dragon\'s blood trees; UNESCO natural site 2008.' } ],
  ['Sohar',24.364,56.746,'Oman','Medieval Omani port (Mazun); traded horses, copper and dates with India and China.','port',
   { old: 'Mazun (Sasanian name of Oman/Sohar)',
     more: 'Sohar on Oman\'s Batinah coast was one of the leading ports of the Indian Ocean in the 9th–10th centuries; the geographer al-Muqaddasi called it the "gateway to China". It exported Omani copper, dates and Arabian horses to India and imported Indian and Chinese goods. Tradition links it with Sinbad the Sailor. The Portuguese held it in the 16th c. Sohar Fort survives; Oman\'s maritime trade links with Gujarat and Malabar date back to Harappan Magan.' } ],
  ['Qal’at al-Bahrain (Dilmun)',26.234,50.521,'Bahrain','Dilmun of Mesopotamian texts; Harappan (Meluhha) weights & seals found — Indus–Gulf trade.','centre',
   { old: 'Dilmun / Tilmun (Sumerian–Akkadian texts); Tylos (Greek); Portuguese Fort',
     more: 'The tell of Qal\'at al-Bahrain, crowned by a 16th-c. Portuguese fort, was the capital of Dilmun, the trading partner of Mesopotamia between c. 2200 and 1600 BCE. Mesopotamian texts mention ships from Dilmun, Magan (Oman) and Meluhha (generally identified with the Indus Civilisation). Excavations found cubical Harappan-standard weights, Indus-style and Gulf seals, and carnelian beads. Burial mounds of Dilmun are also UNESCO-listed (2019). Site UNESCO 2005.' } ],
  ['Siraf',27.665,52.347,'Iran (Bushehr)','Leading Abbasid-era Gulf port (8–10th c.) trading with India and China.','port',
   { old: 'Taheri (modern village); Sīrāf',
     more: 'Siraf on the Iranian Gulf coast was the main port of the Abbasid Persian Gulf in the 8th–10th centuries, from where ships sailed to India, Sri Lanka and Guangzhou in China; the Arabic "Accounts of China and India" (851) describes the route. Its merchants built lavish houses with teak from India. Excavations found Chinese ceramics and a Friday mosque. Siraf declined after an earthquake (977) and the rise of Kish and later Hormuz.' } ],
  ['Hormuz',27.068,56.46,'Iran','Kingdom of Hormuz controlled Gulf trade & horse export to India; Portuguese 1515, taken by Anglo-Persian force 1622.','port',
   { old: 'Old Hormuz (Harmozeia, mainland near Minab) → Jarun island (c. 1300); Ormus (European)',
     more: 'The kingdom of Hormuz moved its capital from the mainland to Jarun island c. 1300 to escape raiders and dominated Gulf trade, especially the export of Arabian and Persian horses to Vijayanagara and the Deccan sultanates. Marco Polo and Abdur Razzaq passed through it. Afonso de Albuquerque took it for Portugal in 1507/1515. In 1622 an Anglo-Persian force (East India Company ships and Shah Abbas I\'s army) captured it, and trade shifted to Bandar Abbas.' } ],
  ['Basra',30.515,47.81,'Iraq','Abbasid port on the Shatt al-Arab; base of the Gulf–India sea trade; Sinbad tales.','port',
   { old: 'Al-Basrah (founded 636 as an Arab garrison misr); near ancient Teredon',
     more: 'Basra was founded by the Rashidun Arabs c. 636 as a garrison town at the head of the Persian Gulf. Under the Abbasids it became a great port for Indian Ocean trade (connecting to Sind, Malabar and China) and an intellectual centre — the Mu\'tazila school, grammarians, al-Jahiz and the Ikhwan al-Safa. The tales of Sinbad the Sailor start from Basra. The Zanj revolt of East African slaves (869–883) devastated it. British forces landed here in WWI (1914), mostly Indian Army troops.' } ],
  ['Banbhore (Debal)',24.751,67.521,'Pakistan (Sindh)','Indus-delta port usually identified with Debal; taken by Muhammad bin Qasim 712; Barbaricum lay in this delta.','port',
   { old: 'Debal / Daybul (Arab sources, identification attributed); Barbarikon (Periplus port in the delta)',
     more: 'Banbhore, east of Karachi, is widely identified with Debal, the port of Sind captured in 712 by the 17-year-old Umayyad general Muhammad bin Qasim, sent by al-Hajjaj after pirates seized Arab ships; he then defeated Raja Dahir at Aror and Brahmanabad. The ruins include one of the earliest mosques in South Asia (727, with Kufic inscriptions). Barbarikon, the Indus-delta port of the Periplus, lay somewhere in the same delta. Tentative UNESCO list.' } ],
  ['Mantai (Ketheeswaram)',8.958,79.963,'Sri Lanka (Mannar)','Ancient port facing India; hub between Arabian Sea and Bay of Bengal trade; Ketheeswaram Shiva temple.','port',
   { old: 'Mahatittha (Pali, "great port"); Mantottam / Mathottam (Tamil); Matota',
     more: 'Mantai, near Mannar opposite Rameswaram, was a major port of the Anuradhapura kingdom from about the 3rd c. BCE to the 10th–11th c. CE, a hub where ships from the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal met. Excavations found Roman, Persian, Islamic and Chinese pottery and semi-precious stones. The nearby Thiruketheeswaram Shiva temple is praised in the Tevaram hymns of Sambandar and Sundarar; the port declined after the Chola invasions of the late 10th c.' } ],
  ['Oc Eo',10.233,105.152,'Vietnam (An Giang)','Funan port (1st–7th c.); Roman coin of Antoninus Pius, Indian seals, Sanskrit inscriptions found.','port',
   { old: 'Port of Funan (Chinese "Fu-nan"); possibly Ptolemy\'s Kattigara (proposed)',
     more: 'Oc Eo in the Mekong delta was a port city of the Funan kingdom (c. 1st–7th c. CE), which Chinese sources say was founded by an Indian Brahmin, Kaundinya, who married a local queen (Soma/Liu Ye). Excavations by Louis Malleret (1944) found a gold coin of Roman emperor Antoninus Pius (152 CE), Indian seals and rings with Brahmi/Sanskrit inscriptions, and Buddhist and Vishnu images. Part of the early "Indianisation" of South-East Asia.' } ],
  ['Malacca',2.194,102.249,'Malaysia','Sultanate (c. 1400) controlling the Strait; Gujarati & Tamil merchants; captured by Portuguese Albuquerque 1511.','port',
   { old: 'Melaka (Malay); Malacca / Malaca (Portuguese)',
     more: 'Founded c. 1400 by Parameswara, a prince from Palembang/Temasek who later converted to Islam, the Malacca Sultanate controlled the Strait of Malacca between India and China. Zheng He\'s Ming fleets used it as a base. Gujarati, Tamil (Kling) and Bengali merchants dominated its trade; Tomé Pires described 84 languages spoken. Afonso de Albuquerque captured it in 1511; the Dutch took it in 1641 and the British in 1795/1824. A Famosa gate survives; UNESCO 2008 (with George Town).' } ],
  ['Guangzhou (Canton)',23.13,113.26,'China','Chief Chinese port for the southern seas since Tang times; Arab, Persian & Indian merchant quarters.','port',
   { old: 'Panyu (Qin–Han); Canton (European, from Guangdong); Khanfu (Arab sources)',
     more: 'Guangzhou on the Pearl River delta was China\'s main southern port from the Han and Tang eras; Arab writers called it Khanfu. A large foreign quarter of Arab, Persian and Indian merchants existed; the Huaisheng Mosque is attributed to the 7th c. Indian monk Bodhidharma traditionally landed nearby. In 878 rebels under Huang Chao massacred foreign merchants. From 1757 it was the only port open to Western traders (Canton System), which fed the opium trade from British India.' } ],
  ['Quanzhou (Zaitun)',24.874,118.676,'China (Fujian)','Marco Polo’s "Zaitun", great Song–Yuan port; Tamil inscription (1281) & Shiva temple remains; UNESCO.','port',
   { old: 'Zaitun / Zayton (Arab & Marco Polo, from Citong "coral tree"); Licheng',
     more: 'Quanzhou in Fujian was the greatest port of Song and Yuan China; Marco Polo (1292) and Ibn Battuta called it Zaitun. Arab, Persian and Tamil merchants lived here: remains of a Hindu Shiva temple and a bilingual Tamil–Chinese inscription of 1281 (period of Kublai Khan) were found, along with mosques (Qingjing, 1009) and Nestorian and Manichaean relics. "Quanzhou: Emporium of the World in Song-Yuan China" is UNESCO-listed (2021).' } ],
  ['Kilwa Kisiwani',-8.96,39.513,'Tanzania','Swahili sultanate; Zimbabwe gold traded for Indian cloth & beads; Ibn Battuta visited 1331; UNESCO.','port',
   { old: 'Kilwa (Swahili "island"); Quíloa (Portuguese)',
     more: 'Kilwa Kisiwani was the leading Swahili city-state of the 13th–15th centuries, controlling the export of gold from Great Zimbabwe via Sofala and minting its own copper coins. It imported Indian cotton cloth, glass beads and Chinese porcelain. Ibn Battuta visited in 1331 and called it one of the most beautiful cities. The Great Mosque and Husuni Kubwa palace survive. Portuguese sacked it in 1505. Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Songo Mnara, UNESCO 1981.' } ],
  ['Zanzibar',-6.165,39.199,'Tanzania','Swahili port for cloves, ivory and slaves; Omani sultanate capital (1840); many Gujarati traders.','port',
   { old: 'Zanzibar from Persian "Zangi-bar" ("coast of the blacks"); Unguja (Swahili name of the island)',
     more: 'Zanzibar was a Swahili trading island that came under Portugal (16th c.) and then Oman. Sultan Seyyid Said moved the Omani capital from Muscat to Stone Town in 1840; it boomed on cloves, ivory and the East African slave trade, financed largely by Gujarati (Kutchi, Bohra, Khoja) merchants. It became a British protectorate in 1890 and fought the shortest war in history (1896). Stone Town is UNESCO-listed (2000); Freddie Mercury was born here to a Parsi family.' } ],
  ['Mombasa — Fort Jesus',-4.063,39.68,'Kenya','Swahili port; Vasco da Gama called 1498 before Malindi; Portuguese Fort Jesus (1593); UNESCO.','port',
   { old: 'Mvita ("island of war", Swahili); Mombaça (Portuguese)',
     more: 'Mombasa was a Swahili port visited by Vasco da Gama in April 1498 on his first voyage; hostile there, he went on to friendly Malindi, where he took an Indian Ocean pilot (once said to be Ahmad ibn Majid, now doubted; often thought Gujarati) to reach Calicut in May 1498. The Portuguese built Fort Jesus (1593–96), designed by Italian architect Giovanni Battista Cairati; Omani Arabs captured it in 1698 after a long siege. Many Indians later came to build the Uganda Railway (from 1896). UNESCO 2011.' } ]
 ]
});

/* ======================= GREATER INDIA ======================= */
GEO.add({
 id:'his-greaterindia', group:'his-world', title:'Indian Cultural Influence in South-East & East Asia', bn:'দক্ষিণ-পূর্ব ও পূর্ব এশিয়ায় ভারতীয় সংস্কৃতি', icon:'🛕', world:true,
 intro:'কোন মন্দির কোন রাজবংশের, হিন্দু না বৌদ্ধ — যেমন আঙ্কোর ভাট (বিষ্ণু, সূর্যবর্মন II), বরোবুদুর (বৌদ্ধ, শৈলেন্দ্র), প্রম্বানন (শিব) — এটাই প্রিলিমসের প্রিয় প্রশ্ন।',
 types:{hindu:['Hindu temple site','#e65100'], bud:['Buddhist site','#f9a825'], cap:['Kingdom capital / royal inscription','#6a1b9a']},
 pts:[
  ['Angkor — Angkor Wat & Angkor Thom',13.413,103.867,'Cambodia (Siem Reap)','Khmer capital; Angkor Wat of Suryavarman II (12th c.) for Vishnu, largest religious monument; Bayon of Jayavarman VII.','hindu',
   { old: 'Yasodharapura (Khmer capital, Yasovarman I, c. 900); Kambuja / Kambujadesa (kingdom); Vrah Vishnuloka (Angkor Wat)',
     more: 'Jayavarman II declared himself chakravartin (devaraja cult) in 802, founding the Khmer Empire of Kambuja. Yasovarman I built the capital Yasodharapura at Angkor. Suryavarman II (r. 1113–c. 1150) built Angkor Wat for Vishnu, the largest religious monument in the world, with reliefs of the churning of the ocean and the Ramayana. Jayavarman VII, a Mahayana Buddhist, built Angkor Thom and the Bayon (late 12th c.). India\'s ASI restored Angkor Wat (1986–93) and Ta Prohm. UNESCO 1992.' } ],
  ['Banteay Srei',13.599,103.963,'Cambodia (Siem Reap)','10th-c. pink sandstone Shiva temple ("citadel of women"), finest Khmer relief carving.','hindu',
   { old: 'Tribhuvanamaheshvara (original dedication); Banteay Srei = "citadel of women / beauty"',
     more: 'Consecrated in 967 to Shiva (as Tribhuvanamaheshvara), Banteay Srei was built not by a king but by the Brahmin courtiers Yajnavaraha and Vishnukumara during the reign of Rajendravarman and Jayavarman V. Its small temples of hard pink sandstone carry the finest deep reliefs in Khmer art, including Ravana shaking Mount Kailasa and the burning of Kama. French conservators first used anastylosis here in the 1930s. Part of the Angkor UNESCO site.' } ],
  ['Sambor Prei Kuk (Isanapura)',12.871,105.043,'Cambodia (Kampong Thom)','Capital of Chenla king Isanavarman I (7th c.); brick Shiva temples; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Ishanapura (capital of Chenla / Zhenla, Chinese sources)',
     more: 'Sambor Prei Kuk is identified with Ishanapura, capital of the pre-Angkorian Chenla kingdom under Isanavarman I (early 7th c.), who absorbed Funan. Over 100 brick temples, mainly for Shiva (and Harihara), have octagonal towers and "flying palace" reliefs, and Sanskrit and Old Khmer inscriptions survive. The Sambor Prei Kuk style shaped later Khmer art. The Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang referred to "Isanapura". UNESCO World Heritage Site 2017.' } ],
  ['Preah Vihear',14.391,104.68,'Cambodia (Thai border)','Khmer Shiva temple on a Dangrek cliff (11th c.); ICJ gave it to Cambodia in 1962; UNESCO.','hindu',
   { old: 'Sri Sikharesvara ("Lord of the Peak", Shiva); Khao Phra Wihan (Thai)',
     more: 'Built mainly in the 11th–12th centuries under Suryavarman I and Suryavarman II, Preah Vihear is a Shiva temple of five gopuras rising along a 800 m axis to a cliff edge on the Dangrek escarpment. Thailand and Cambodia disputed it; the International Court of Justice awarded it to Cambodia in 1962, and in 2013 clarified that the surrounding promontory is Cambodian. Clashes followed its UNESCO listing in 2008. Classic case for IR and boundary law.' } ],
  ['Wat Phu',14.848,105.822,'Laos (Champasak)','Khmer Shiva temple (5th–13th c.) below the Lingaparvata peak; UNESCO.','hindu',
   { old: 'Vat Phou / Lingaparvata temple; Shrestapura (nearby ancient city, attributed)',
     more: 'Wat Phu in Champasak lies at the foot of Phou Kao mountain, whose peak resembles a linga, which made it sacred to Shiva. A temple existed from the 5th century near the city of Shrestapura, linked to early Chenla; the present structures date mainly to the 11th–13th centuries. A road connected it to Angkor. The landscape plan aligning mountain, temple, river and city reflects Hindu cosmology. Later converted to Theravada worship. UNESCO 2001.' } ],
  ['Luang Prabang',19.89,102.135,'Laos','Royal capital of Lan Xang; Theravada wats; named after the Phra Bang Buddha image; UNESCO.','bud',
   { old: 'Muang Sua → Xieng Thong (Xiang Dong Xiang Thong) → Luang Prabang (from the Phra Bang image)',
     more: 'Fa Ngum founded the Lao kingdom of Lan Xang ("million elephants") here in 1353 with Khmer support and brought the Phra Bang Buddha image from Angkor, which later gave the city its name. It remained the royal capital after the political capital moved to Vientiane (1560) and stayed the seat of the Lao kings until 1975. Wat Xieng Thong (1560) is the finest temple; monks\' morning alms round is famous. UNESCO 1995.' } ],
  ['Mỹ Sơn',15.764,108.124,'Vietnam (Quảng Nam)','Champa’s Shiva sanctuary (4th–13th c.), begun with Bhadravarman’s Bhadreshvara linga; UNESCO.','hindu',
   { old: 'Champa\'s sanctuary of Bhadreshvara; Champa capital Simhapura (Trà Kiệu) nearby; Amaravati region',
     more: 'Mỹ Sơn, in a valley near the Cham capital Simhapura, was the main religious centre of Champa from the 4th to 13th century. King Bhadravarman (late 4th c.) founded a wooden temple to Shiva as Bhadreshvara, linking royal power to the god; later kings rebuilt it in brick. About 70 brick towers stood until US bombing in 1969 destroyed many. India\'s ASI has restored temple groups since 2017. Sanskrit inscriptions in Brahmi-derived script. UNESCO 1999.' } ],
  ['Po Nagar',12.265,109.196,'Vietnam (Nha Trang)','Cham temple (7th–12th c.) of goddess Yan Po Nagar, identified with Bhagavati / Uma.','hindu',
   { old: 'Kauthara (Cham principality); Yan Po Nagar = "Lady of the Kingdom"',
     more: 'The Po Nagar towers on a hill at the mouth of the Cai river in Nha Trang were built between the 7th and 12th centuries in the Cham principality of Kauthara. The main tower houses a ten-armed image of the goddess Yan Po Nagar, identified in Sanskrit inscriptions with Bhagavati, Uma or Mahishasuramardini, consort of Shiva. An inscription of 781 records the reconstruction after Javanese raiders burned the temple in 774. Still worshipped by Cham and Vietnamese.' } ],
  ['Bagan',21.172,94.86,'Myanmar','Pagan kingdom capital (Anawrahta, 1044); 2,000+ Theravada temples incl. Ananda; UNESCO 2019.','bud',
   { old: 'Pagan; Arimaddanapura ("city of the enemy crusher", Pali); Tambadipa',
     more: 'Bagan was the capital of the first Burmese empire founded by King Anawrahta (r. 1044–1077), who adopted Theravada Buddhism after conquering the Mon city of Thaton with help from the monk Shin Arahan. Between the 11th and 13th centuries over 10,000 temples were built; about 2,200 survive, including Ananda (Kyanzittha, 1105), Shwezigon and Dhammayangyi. Kublai\'s Mongol attacks (1287) ended the empire. Bodh Gaya\'s Mahabodhi was copied at Bagan. UNESCO 2019.' } ],
  ['Sri Ksetra',18.81,95.288,'Myanmar (near Pyay)','Largest Pyu city (5th–9th c.); Pali & Pyu inscriptions; part of Pyu Ancient Cities, UNESCO 2014.','cap',
   { old: 'Sri Ksetra ("field of fortune"); Thayekhittaya (Burmese); Pyu city-state',
     more: 'Sri Ksetra near Pyay was the largest of the Pyu city-states of the Irrawaddy valley (c. 5th–9th c. CE), enclosed by a circular brick wall. Finds include the earliest Pali texts in South-East Asia (the gold leaf Khin Ba manuscript), Pyu inscriptions in a South Indian (Kadamba-type) script, and the cylindrical Bawbawgyi stupa. Nanzhao raids ended the Pyu in the 9th c. "Pyu Ancient Cities" (Halin, Beikthano, Sri Ksetra) — Myanmar\'s first UNESCO site, 2014.' } ],
  ['Shwedagon Pagoda',16.798,96.15,'Myanmar (Yangon)','Gilded stupa said to enshrine Buddha’s hair relics; holiest Buddhist site in Myanmar.','bud',
   { old: 'Singuttara hill; Dagon (old name of Yangon) → Rangoon (British) → Yangon (1989)',
     more: 'Tradition says the brothers Tapussa and Bhallika, merchants who gave the Buddha his first meal after enlightenment, brought eight of his hairs to Dagon, where they were enshrined. Historians date the stupa to between the 6th and 10th centuries. Mon queen Shin Sawbu greatly enlarged it in the 15th c.; the 99 m golden stupa is topped by a diamond-studded orb. It was the rallying point of the 1988 democracy movement (Aung San Suu Kyi spoke here).' } ],
  ['Sukhothai',17.021,99.704,'Thailand','First Thai kingdom (13th c.); Ram Khamhaeng inscription & Thai script; Theravada Buddhism; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Sukhodaya ("dawn of happiness", Sanskrit-Pali)',
     more: 'Sukhothai, founded c. 1238 by Si Inthrathit after breaking away from Khmer control, is regarded as the first Thai kingdom. King Ram Khamhaeng (late 13th c.) is credited with inventing the Thai script; his 1292 stone inscription (authenticity debated by some scholars) describes a just Buddhist king. Sukhothai adopted Sri Lankan Theravada Buddhism and developed the "walking Buddha" style. It became a vassal of Ayutthaya in 1438. UNESCO 1991 (with Si Satchanalai and Kamphaeng Phet).' } ],
  ['Ayutthaya',14.35,100.583,'Thailand','Siamese capital 1351–1767, named after Ayodhya; Ramakien tradition; sacked by the Burmese 1767; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Krung Si Ayutthaya / Dvaravati Sri Ayutthaya (after Ayodhya); Siam (European name of the kingdom)',
     more: 'Founded in 1351 by King U Thong (Ramathibodi I) on an island at the junction of three rivers, Ayutthaya was the capital of Siam for over four centuries and one of the world\'s great trading cities, with Portuguese, Dutch, Japanese, Persian and Indian quarters. Named after Rama\'s Ayodhya, its kings bore the title Rama, and the Ramakien became the Thai national epic. The Burmese Konbaung army sacked it in 1767; Bangkok later became capital. UNESCO 1991.' } ],
  ['Nakhon Pathom — Phra Pathom Chedi',13.82,100.06,'Thailand','Dvaravati Mon Buddhist centre; tallest stupa; tradition links it to Ashoka’s monks Sona & Uttara.','bud',
   { old: 'Nakhon Chai Si; Nagara Pathama ("first city"); Dvaravati centre',
     more: 'Nakhon Pathom was a major centre of the Mon Dvaravati culture (c. 6th–11th c.), where some of the earliest Buddhist remains of Thailand, including Wheels of the Law (dharmachakras) and Pali inscriptions, were found. Tradition links the arrival of Buddhism to the monks Sona and Uttara sent by Ashoka to Suvarnabhumi after the Third Buddhist Council. The present Phra Pathom Chedi (about 120 m), the tallest stupa in the world, was built by King Mongkut (Rama IV) over an older stupa in the 19th c.' } ],
  ['Bujang Valley',5.738,100.414,'Malaysia (Kedah)','Kedah (Kataha / Kadaram) candi ruins; Hindu-Buddhist port raided by Chola Rajendra I, 1025.','hindu',
   { old: 'Kataha / Kadaram / Kidaram (Tamil & Sanskrit); Kalah (Arab sources, attributed); Kedah',
     more: 'The Bujang Valley in Kedah holds over 50 candi (temple) remains dating from about the 5th to 14th centuries, plus the Sungai Batu iron-smelting site possibly from the 1st c. CE. As Kadaram, it was an important port of the Srivijaya realm and is named in the Tanjore inscription of Rajendra Chola I among places conquered during his naval expedition of c. 1025. Finds include Buddhist inscriptions (Buddhagupta stone), linga-yoni and Chinese ceramics.' } ],
  ['Palembang — Bukit Seguntang',-2.997,104.726,'Indonesia (Sumatra)','Srivijaya capital; I-Tsing studied here (671); Balaputradeva built a Nalanda vihara (Devapala’s grant); Chola raid 1025.','cap',
   { old: 'Srivijaya (Sri Bhoja / Shih-li-fo-shih in Chinese); Sribuza / Zabag (Arab sources)',
     more: 'Palembang on the Musi river is generally accepted as the main centre of Srivijaya (7th–13th c.), the maritime Buddhist empire that controlled the Malacca Strait. Its Kedukan Bukit inscription (682, Old Malay in Pallava script) records the founding by Dapunta Hyang. The Chinese monk I-Tsing studied Sanskrit here in 671 before Nalanda. Devapala\'s Nalanda copper plate records a grant for a monastery built by Srivijaya king Balaputradeva. Rajendra Chola raided it c. 1025.' } ],
  ['Borobudur',-7.608,110.204,'Indonesia (Central Java)','Largest Buddhist monument; Sailendra dynasty (c. 8th–9th c.) Mahayana stupa-mandala; UNESCO.','bud',
   { old: 'Bhumisambharabhudhara ("mountain of virtues of the ten stages", inscription, attributed); Kamulan',
     more: 'Built c. 780–830 by the Sailendra dynasty, Borobudur is the world\'s largest Buddhist monument, a stepped stupa-mandala of nine platforms topped by a central stupa and 72 perforated stupas. Pilgrims walk clockwise past 2,672 relief panels showing karma, the Jataka tales, the Buddha\'s life and the Gandavyuha. It was buried by volcanic ash and jungle and rediscovered in 1814 under Stamford Raffles. Restored by UNESCO (1975–82). UNESCO World Heritage Site 1991.' } ],
  ['Prambanan',-7.752,110.492,'Indonesia (Central Java)','9th-c. Trimurti temples (Shiva the largest) of Mataram (Sanjaya) kings; Ramayana reliefs; UNESCO.','hindu',
   { old: 'Shivagrha ("House of Shiva", Shivagrha inscription 856); Rara Jonggrang (Javanese legend)',
     more: 'Prambanan, consecrated in 856 according to the Shivagrha inscription, was built by Rakai Pikatan of the Sanjaya line of the Mataram kingdom, probably in response to the Buddhist Borobudur. The central shrine of Shiva (47 m) is flanked by those of Brahma and Vishnu (Trimurti), facing temples for their vahanas. Its balustrades carry Ramayana and Krishnayana reliefs, still the setting for Ramayana ballet. Damaged by an earthquake in 2006. UNESCO 1991.' } ],
  ['Dieng Plateau',-7.2,109.9,'Indonesia (Central Java)','Oldest Javanese Hindu temples (Arjuna group, 7th–8th c.), dedicated to Shiva.','hindu',
   { old: 'Di Hyang ("abode of the gods", Old Javanese)',
     more: 'The Dieng Plateau, a volcanic caldera at about 2,000 m, holds the oldest standing Hindu stone temples of Java (c. late 7th–early 8th c.), dedicated to Shiva. Originally there were over 400 temples; eight survive, named after Mahabharata heroes by later Javanese (Arjuna, Srikandi, Puntadewa, Semar, Bima). The Candi Bima tower shows Indian Nagara-style influence. An early inscription dates to 809.' } ],
  ['Ciaruteun inscription',-6.525,106.668,'Indonesia (Bogor, W Java)','5th-c. Sanskrit inscription in Pallava script of Purnavarman of Tarumanagara, with his footprints.','cap',
   { old: 'Tarumanagara (kingdom of Purnavarman); Ciaruteun stone',
     more: 'The Ciaruteun inscription, a boulder in a river near Bogor, carries four lines of Sanskrit verse in Pallava (South Indian) script praising King Purnavarman of Tarumanagara, comparing his two carved footprints to those of Vishnu. It dates to about the 5th century CE and, with the Tugu inscription (canal digging), is among the earliest records of a Hindu kingdom in Java. The Chinese pilgrim Faxian (414) noted Brahmanism dominant in "Yepoti" (Java).' } ],
  ['Besakih',-8.374,115.451,'Indonesia (Bali)','"Mother temple" of Balinese Hinduism on the slopes of Mt Agung.','hindu',
   { old: 'Pura Penataran Agung (main temple); Basukih ("Vasuki", the naga) attributed',
     more: 'Pura Besakih on the south-west slope of Mount Agung, Bali\'s highest volcano, is a complex of over 80 temples and the most important temple of Balinese Hinduism. Its origins are traditionally traced to the 8th c. sage Markandeya, and it became the state temple of the Gelgel kingdom (15th c.). The three main shrines honour Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva. It escaped the 1963 eruption that killed about 1,100 people. Bali preserved Hindu-Javanese culture after Majapahit fell.' } ],
  ['Anuradhapura',8.335,80.411,'Sri Lanka','Capital c. 4th c. BCE–11th c.; Sri Maha Bodhi from the Bodh Gaya sapling brought by Sanghamitra; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Anuradhagama (Mahavamsa); Anurogrammum (Ptolemy)',
     more: 'Anuradhapura was the capital of Sri Lanka\'s first kingdom for over 1,300 years (c. 4th c. BCE–1017 CE). King Devanampiya Tissa accepted Buddhism from Ashoka\'s son Mahinda (c. 250 BCE); Ashoka\'s daughter Sanghamitta brought a sapling of the Bodh Gaya tree, the Sri Maha Bodhi, oldest documented planted tree. Great stupas include Ruwanwelisaya, Jetavanaramaya and Abhayagiri. The city fell to Rajaraja Chola I\'s invasion (993) and was abandoned for Polonnaruwa. UNESCO 1982.' } ],
  ['Sigiriya',7.957,80.76,'Sri Lanka','Rock-palace of King Kashyapa I (5th c.); maiden frescoes, Mirror Wall graffiti; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Sihagiri / Simhagiri ("Lion Rock")',
     more: 'King Kashyapa I (r. 477–495), who killed his father Dhatusena, built a palace on top of a 200 m rock, entered through a giant lion\'s paws. The rock face retains frescoes of celestial maidens (apsaras), compared to Ajanta paintings, and the polished Mirror Wall bears graffiti poems of the 8th–10th centuries in Sinhala. Kashyapa\'s brother Moggallana defeated him and the site became a monastery. Landscaped water gardens are among the oldest in Asia. UNESCO 1982.' } ],
  ['Polonnaruwa',7.933,81.0,'Sri Lanka','Capital after Chola conquest by Rajaraja I; Chola Shiva Devales; later Parakramabahu I; UNESCO.','cap',
   { old: 'Jananathamangalam (Chola name, 11th c.); Pulatthinagara / Pulastipura',
     more: 'Rajaraja Chola I conquered northern Sri Lanka c. 993 and made Polonnaruwa the Chola capital, renamed Jananathamangalam; Shiva Devale No. 2 is a Chola stone temple. Vijayabahu I expelled the Cholas in 1070. Parakramabahu I (r. 1153–1186) built the vast Parakrama Samudra reservoir, unified the Buddhist sangha and sent an expedition against Pandya Madurai. The Gal Vihara rock-cut Buddhas date to his reign. UNESCO 1982.' } ],
  ['Kandy — Temple of the Tooth',7.294,80.641,'Sri Lanka','Last Sinhalese kingdom (fell to British 1815); Dalada Maligawa enshrines Buddha’s tooth relic; UNESCO.','bud',
   { old: 'Senkadagala / Senkadagalapura; Mahanuvara ("great city", Sinhala); Kanda Uda Rata',
     more: 'Kandy was the capital of the last independent Sinhalese kingdom, which survived Portuguese and Dutch attacks in its hill country for over 200 years. The Buddha\'s tooth relic, a symbol of the right to rule, has been kept in the Sri Dalada Maligawa since the late 16th c.; it was brought to Sri Lanka in the 4th c. from Kalinga according to tradition. The British took Kandy in 1815; the Kandyan Convention ended the kingdom. The Esala Perahera procession is held annually. UNESCO 1988.' } ],
  ['Lhasa — Jokhang & Potala',29.658,91.117,'China (Tibet)','Jokhang founded under Songtsen Gampo (7th c.); Potala, seat of the Dalai Lamas; Tibetan Buddhism drew on Indian masters.','bud',
   { old: 'Rasa ("enclosure of goats", early name); Lhasa = "place of the gods"',
     more: 'Songtsen Gampo (7th c.) unified Tibet and made Lhasa his capital; his Nepali queen Bhrikuti and Chinese queen Wencheng are credited with bringing Buddha images to the Jokhang and Ramoche temples. Tibetan script was devised from an Indian (Gupta-derived) script by Thonmi Sambhota, sent to India. The 5th Dalai Lama built the Potala Palace (from 1645) on Red Hill. The 14th Dalai Lama fled to India in 1959 (Dharamshala). Historic Ensemble of the Potala Palace, UNESCO 1994.' } ],
  ['Nara — Tōdai-ji',34.689,135.84,'Japan','Great Buddha (Vairocana) hall; eye-opening ceremony (752) performed by the Indian monk Bodhisena.','bud',
   { old: 'Heijō-kyō (capital 710–784); Yamato',
     more: 'Nara (Heijō-kyō), modelled on Tang Chang\'an, was Japan\'s capital from 710 to 784. Emperor Shōmu built Tōdai-ji with a huge bronze Vairocana Buddha (Daibutsu) to protect the state. At the eye-opening ceremony in 752, the Indian monk Bodhisena (Bodhisena of Jambudvipa, who came via China in 736) painted in the pupils. The Shōsōin repository keeps treasures from the Silk Road, including Indian and Persian objects. Historic Monuments of Ancient Nara, UNESCO 1998.' } ],
  ['Gyeongju — Bulguksa',35.79,129.332,'South Korea','Silla capital; Bulguksa temple & Seokguram grotto (8th c.) of Unified Silla Buddhism; UNESCO.','bud',
   { old: 'Seorabeol / Gyerim / Geumseong (Silla capital names)',
     more: 'Gyeongju was the capital of the Silla kingdom for nearly a thousand years (57 BCE–935 CE); Silla unified most of Korea in 668 with Tang help. Buddhism had reached Korea in the 4th c. Under minister Kim Dae-seong, Bulguksa temple and the Seokguram grotto, with its granite seated Buddha, were begun in 751. The Silla monk Hyecho travelled to India in the 720s and wrote "Memoir of a Pilgrimage to the Five Kingdoms of India". UNESCO 1995.' } ],
  ['Luoyang — White Horse Temple',34.724,112.6,'China (Henan)','Traditionally China’s first Buddhist temple (68 CE, Han Mingdi); Indian monks Kashyapa Matanga & Dharmaratna.','bud',
   { old: 'Luoyi / Chengzhou (Zhou); Luoyang (Eastern Han capital); Baima Si = White Horse Temple',
     more: 'Tradition says Han emperor Ming (Mingdi) dreamt of a golden man and sent envoys west; they returned in 67/68 CE with the Indian monks Kashyapa Matanga and Dharmaratna, carrying sutras and images on white horses. The emperor built the White Horse Temple at his capital Luoyang, traditionally China\'s first Buddhist temple; the monks translated the "Sutra of Forty-two Chapters". Their tombs lie in the temple. India built a Buddhist hall here (2010). Longmen Grottoes nearby, UNESCO.' } ]
 ]
});

