GEO.add({
 id:'his-buddhism', group:'his-world', title:'Spread of Buddhism & Foreign Travellers', bn:'বৌদ্ধধর্মের প্রসার ও বিদেশি পর্যটক', icon:'☸', world:true,
 intro:'পর্যটক → কোন শাসকের সময় ভারতে এসেছিলেন — এই জোড়াটা মুখস্থ রাখো (ফা-হিয়েন: চন্দ্রগুপ্ত II, হিউয়েন সাং: হর্ষ, ইবন বতুতা: মুহম্মদ বিন তুঘলক, পায়েস: কৃষ্ণদেব রায়)। পিন বসানো পর্যটকের নিজের শহরে।',
 types:{bud:['Buddhist centre abroad','#f9a825'], trav:['Foreign traveller (home city)','#1565c0']},
 pts:[
  ['Lumbini — Maya Devi Temple',27.47,83.276,'Nepal (Rupandehi)','Buddha’s birthplace (c. 6th/5th c. BCE); Ashoka’s pillar inscription (c. 249 BCE); UNESCO.','bud',
   { old: 'Lumbini-vana ("Lummini gama" in Ashoka\'s pillar inscription); Rummindei / Rupandehi',
     more: 'Lumbini is the garden where Queen Mahamaya gave birth to Siddhartha Gautama. Ashoka visited in his 20th regnal year (c. 249 BCE) and set up a pillar whose Brahmi inscription says "here the Buddha, the Sakyamuni, was born" and that the village was exempted from tax (bali) and paid only one-eighth (bhaga). Faxian and Xuanzang described the site. The pillar was found in 1896 by Alois Führer and Khadga Shumsher. UNESCO 1997; India and Nepal cooperate on a Buddhist circuit.' } ],
  ['Kapilavastu (Tilaurakot)',27.577,83.055,'Nepal (Kapilvastu)','Ruins identified with the Shakya capital where Siddhartha grew up; rival claim Piprahwa (India).','bud',
   { old: 'Kapilavatthu (Pali); Kapilavastu (Sanskrit); capital of the Sakya gana',
     more: 'Kapilavastu was the capital of the Sakya republic (gana-sangha), ruled by Suddhodana, where Siddhartha lived until his renunciation at 29. Nepal identifies it with the walled site of Tilaurakot near Taulihawa; India identifies it with Piprahwa (Siddharthnagar district, UP), where a casket inscription referring to relics of the Buddha of the Sakyas was found in 1898 and a seal "Kapilavastu" was excavated in the 1970s. The Sakyas were later massacred by Kosala\'s Vidudabha.' } ],
  ['Taxila — Jaulian Monastery',33.766,72.875,'Pakistan (Haripur)','Gandharan monastery & stupas (2nd–5th c.) at Taxila, a great centre visited by Faxian; UNESCO.','bud',
   { old: 'Takshashila (Sanskrit); Taxila (Greek); Takkasila (Pali); Chu-cha-shi-lo (Faxian)',
     more: 'Taxila, between the Indus and Jhelum, was a capital of Gandhara and famed centre of learning (traditionally Chanakya and Panini, physician Jivaka). Its successive cities are Bhir Mound (Achaemenid–Mauryan), Sirkap (Indo-Greek–Parthian) and Sirsukh (Kushan). Ambhi submitted to Alexander here (326 BCE); Ashoka was viceroy here. Jaulian monastery (2nd–5th c.) has stucco Buddhas and a "healing Buddha". Excavated by John Marshall (1913–34). UNESCO 1980.' } ],
  ['Takht-i-Bahi',34.286,71.947,'Pakistan (Mardan)','Gandharan hilltop monastery (from 1st c. CE), linked to Indo-Parthian Gondophares; UNESCO.','bud',
   { old: 'Takht-i-Bahi = "throne of origins / springs"; Gondophares inscription (Takht-i-Bahi stone)',
     more: 'The Takht-i-Bahi monastery, on a hilltop near Mardan, was founded in the 1st century CE and occupied until the 7th c.; its stupa court, monks\' cells, assembly hall and Gandhara sculptures are among the best preserved. An inscription found here dated in year 103 of an era and the 26th year of the Indo-Parthian king Gondophares (Gudufara), whom Christian tradition links with St Thomas, helps date the period. UNESCO 1980 with the remains of Sahr-i-Bahlol.' } ],
  ['Swat (Uddiyana) — Butkara',34.757,72.362,'Pakistan (Mingora)','Uddiyana of Faxian & Xuanzang; Butkara I stupa (Mauryan origin); traditional homeland of Padmasambhava.','bud',
   { old: 'Uddiyana / Udyana ("garden"); Suvastu (Rigvedic river name); Wu-chang (Faxian)',
     more: 'The Swat valley was the ancient Uddiyana, a major Buddhist land visited by Faxian (c. 403) and Xuanzang, who reported 1,400 monasteries, many deserted. Butkara I, excavated by Italian archaeologist Domenico Faccenna (1956–62), is a large stupa with a Mauryan-period core enlarged five times, and many Gandhara sculptures. Tibetan tradition holds that Padmasambhava, who took Vajrayana Buddhism to Tibet in the 8th c., was born in Uddiyana. Udegram has a Ghaznavid-era mosque.' } ],
  ['Peshawar — Kanishka Stupa',33.999,71.592,'Pakistan (Shah-ji-ki-Dheri)','Purushapura, Kushan capital; Kanishka’s great stupa and reliquary casket (found 1908–09).','bud',
   { old: 'Purushapura (Kushan capital); Peshawar (Akbar\'s name, "frontier town", attributed)',
     more: 'Purushapura was the capital of the Kushan emperor Kanishka (c. 127–150 CE), patron of Mahayana Buddhism and the Fourth Buddhist Council (held in Kashmir). He built a giant stupa here, described by Faxian and Xuanzang as one of the tallest structures in the ancient world. Excavations at Shah-ji-ki-Dheri in 1908–09 by D. B. Spooner found the Kanishka casket, a bronze reliquary with a Kharosthi inscription and images of Kanishka, the Buddha and Brahma and Indra.' } ],
  ['Hadda',34.362,70.471,'Afghanistan (Nangarhar)','Gandharan monasteries near Jalalabad; Greco-Buddhist stucco; Faxian saw the Buddha’s skull relic here.','bud',
   { old: 'Hilo / Hi-lo (Faxian & Xuanzang); Nagarahara region (ancient Jalalabad)',
     more: 'Hadda, near Jalalabad in ancient Nagarahara, was a major Gandharan monastic centre of about the 1st–7th centuries CE, with more than 20 stupas and monasteries. Its stucco and clay sculptures, mixing Greco-Roman and Indian elements (e.g. the Buddha with Heracles-like Vajrapani at Tapa Shotor), are celebrated examples of Greco-Buddhist art. Faxian (c. 402) saw the Buddha\'s skull bone relic worshipped here. Much was destroyed during the Afghan wars (c. 1980s–90s).' } ],
  ['Mes Aynak',34.271,69.307,'Afghanistan (Logar)','Large Kushan-era Buddhist monastery complex (c. 1st–7th c.) above a huge copper deposit.','bud',
   { old: 'Mes Aynak = "little copper well" (Pashto/Dari)',
     more: 'Mes Aynak, south-east of Kabul, is a vast Buddhist site of the Kushan and later periods (c. 1st–8th c.) with fortified monasteries, stupas, statues, frescoes and manuscripts spread over hundreds of hectares, plus ancient copper mines and smelting workshops. It sits on one of the world\'s largest untapped copper deposits, leased to a Chinese state consortium (MCC) in 2007; the planned open-pit mine raised fears of destroying the site, prompting rescue excavations since 2009.' } ],
  ['Bamiyan Buddhas',34.832,67.827,'Afghanistan','Two giant 6th-c. Buddhas (55 m & 38 m) described by Xuanzang (630); blown up by the Taliban, Mar 2001; UNESCO.','bud',
   { old: 'Bamiyan / Bamian; Fan-yen-na (Xuanzang); Salsal & Shahmama (local names of the statues)',
     more: 'Two colossal standing Buddhas, about 55 m (western, Salsal) and 38 m (eastern, Shahmama), were carved into cliffs of the Bamiyan valley in the 6th century (radiocarbon c. 544–644 CE), surrounded by painted caves. Xuanzang saw them gilded and painted in 630. The Taliban blew them up in March 2001, drawing worldwide condemnation. The surrounding cave paintings include some of the world\'s earliest oil paintings. Cultural Landscape and Archaeological Remains of the Bamiyan Valley, UNESCO 2003.' } ],
  ['Termez — Fayaz Tepe',37.286,67.188,'Uzbekistan','Kushan-era Buddhist monastery on the Oxus; with Kara Tepe shows Buddhism’s spread into Central Asia.','bud',
   { old: 'Tarmita / Taramita (Bactrian, Kushan); Ta-mi (Xuanzang); Demetrias (Greek, attributed)',
     more: 'Termez on the Amu Darya (Oxus) was a key crossing point between Bactria and Sogdiana and a major Buddhist centre under the Kushans. Fayaz Tepe (1st–3rd c. CE) is a monastery with a stupa, courtyard and wall paintings; a Buddha triad statue under a bodhi tree is in the Tashkent museum. Nearby Kara Tepe has cave monasteries with Brahmi and Kharosthi inscriptions. Xuanzang (630) recorded about ten monasteries and 1,000 monks here.' } ],
  ['Kucha — Kizil Caves',41.784,82.505,'China (Xinjiang)','Kucha was home of translator Kumarajiva (344–413); Kizil: earliest major Buddhist cave complex in China.','bud',
   { old: 'Kuchi / Qiuci (Chinese); Kucha (Tocharian B kingdom)',
     more: 'Kucha was a Buddhist oasis kingdom on the northern Tarim route, where an Indo-European language (Tocharian B) was spoken. The great translator Kumarajiva (344–413), son of an Indian father and a Kuchean princess, was born here; he was taken to Chang\'an, where he translated the Lotus Sutra and Diamond Sutra into Chinese. The Kizil Caves (3rd–8th c.), with about 236 caves and Indian-Iranian influenced murals, are the earliest major Buddhist cave complex in China. UNESCO Silk Roads, 2014.' } ],
  ['Shaolin Monastery',34.508,112.935,'China (Henan)','Founded 495; tradition credits Bodhidharma, an Indian monk, with starting Chan (Zen) here.','bud',
   { old: 'Shaolin Si ("monastery in the young forest" of Mount Shaoshi)',
     more: 'Emperor Xiaowen of the Northern Wei founded the Shaolin Monastery in 495 on Mount Song for the Indian monk Batuo (Buddhabhadra). Tradition says Bodhidharma, a South Indian monk (often linked to the Pallava region), meditated in a nearby cave for nine years and founded Chan (later Zen) Buddhism; the story is largely legendary. Shaolin monks became famous for martial arts (kung fu). Part of the "Historic Monuments of Dengfeng" UNESCO site (2010).' } ],
  ['Samye',29.326,91.504,'China (Tibet)','First Buddhist monastery in Tibet (c. 779), founded with India’s Shantarakshita & Padmasambhava.','bud',
   { old: 'Samye ("inconceivable"); Bsam-yas',
     more: 'Samye, south-east of Lhasa, was the first Buddhist monastery in Tibet, founded c. 779 by King Trisong Detsen with the Indian abbot Shantarakshita of Nalanda and the tantric master Padmasambhava (Guru Rinpoche), who, according to tradition, subdued local spirits. Its layout is a mandala of Mount Meru, reportedly modelled on Odantapuri in Bihar. The first Tibetan monks were ordained here, and the Samye debate (c. 792–794) favoured Kamalashila\'s gradual Indian path over Chinese Chan.' } ],
  ['Tholing',31.484,79.798,'China (Ngari, W Tibet)','Guge monastery (996) of Rinchen Zangpo; Atisha Dipankara of Vikramashila came here in 1042.','bud',
   { old: 'Thöling / Toling / Zanda (Tsamda county); Guge kingdom',
     more: 'Tholing (Thöling) monastery in Ngari was founded c. 996–997 by Yeshe-Ö, king of Guge, with the translator Rinchen Zangpo, who had studied in Kashmir; it marked the "second diffusion" of Buddhism in Tibet. At the invitation of the Guge kings, the Bengali scholar Atisha Dipankara Srijnana of Vikramashila reached western Tibet in 1042 and stayed at Tholing; his work founded the Kadampa school. The ruins of Tsaparang, the Guge capital, lie nearby. Close to the Indian border (Himachal–Uttarakhand).' } ],
  ['Mihintale',8.351,80.517,'Sri Lanka','Where Mahinda, Ashoka’s son, converted King Devanampiya Tissa (c. 250 BCE); cradle of Sri Lankan Buddhism.','bud',
   { old: 'Missaka Pabbata / Cetiyagiri (Pali, Mahavamsa); Sagiri',
     more: 'Mihintale, a hill east of Anuradhapura, is where, according to the Mahavamsa, the monk Mahinda, son of Ashoka, met King Devanampiya Tissa while he was hunting on Poson full moon day (c. 250 BCE) and converted him to Buddhism. It is regarded as the birthplace of Sri Lankan Buddhism; the Ambasthala Dagoba marks the meeting place and the Mahaseya Dagoba is said to hold a relic of the Buddha. Poson Poya festival is celebrated here each June.' } ],
  ['Megasthenes — Seleucia',33.094,44.522,'Iraq','Envoy of Seleucus I (capital Seleucia on the Tigris) to Chandragupta Maurya at Pataliputra; wrote Indica.','trav',
   { old: 'Seleucia on the Tigris (founded c. 305 BCE by Seleucus I); later twin of Ctesiphon (Veh-Ardashir)',
     more: 'Seleucus I Nicator founded Seleucia on the Tigris c. 305 BCE as his eastern capital. After a war with Chandragupta Maurya (c. 305–303 BCE), he ceded Arachosia, Gedrosia and Paropamisadae for 500 war elephants and sent Megasthenes as ambassador to Pataliputra (Palibothra). Megasthenes\' Indica, surviving in fragments quoted by Arrian, Strabo and Diodorus, describes seven castes, the city\'s wooden walls and administration. Deimachus later went to Bindusara\'s court.' } ],
  ['Fa-Hien (Faxian) — Pingyang',36.088,111.519,'China (Linfen, Shanxi)','Home region (c.); journey 399–412 CE, in India under Chandragupta II; Foguoji; sailed home via Sri Lanka & Java.','trav',
   { old: 'Pingyang (Jin–Northern Wei name of Linfen); Wuyang (Faxian\'s home county, attributed)',
     more: 'Faxian, a Chinese monk from Pingyang (Shanxi), left Chang\'an in 399 to collect Vinaya texts, crossing the Gobi and Hindu Kush. He spent c. 402–411 in India during the reign of Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya), whom he never names, and praised the peaceful, prosperous Gupta realm, mild punishments and charitable hospitals at Pataliputra; he noted Chandalas lived outside towns. He studied at Pataliputra and Tamralipti, then sailed via Sri Lanka and Java, returning in 412. His account is Foguoji (Record of Buddhist Kingdoms).' } ],
  ['Hiuen Tsang — Big Wild Goose Pagoda',34.22,108.959,'China (Xi’an)','Xuanzang left Chang’an 629; studied at Nalanda; at Harsha’s court (Kannauj, Prayag); Si-Yu-Ki; texts kept here.','trav',
   { old: 'Dayan Ta; Ci\'en Temple pagoda (652); Xuanzang = Hiuen Tsang / Yuan Chwang',
     more: 'Xuanzang left Chang\'an secretly in 629, travelled via Turfan, Samarkand and Bamiyan, and spent over a decade in India (c. 630–643), studying under Silabhadra at Nalanda. He attended Harsha\'s assemblies at Kannauj and Prayag (643) and met Bhaskaravarman of Kamarupa; he mentions Pulakeshin II. He returned in 645 with 657 texts. The Big Wild Goose Pagoda (652) was built to store them; his Si-Yu-Ki (Great Tang Records on the Western Regions) is a key source on 7th-c. India. UNESCO Silk Roads, 2014.' } ],
  ['I-Tsing (Yijing) — Qizhou',36.67,117.021,'China (Jinan, Shandong)','Home (attributed); sailed via Srivijaya; at Nalanda c. 675–685; recorded Buddhist monastic practice.','trav',
   { old: 'Qizhou (Tang prefecture, modern Jinan); Licheng (attributed home county); Fanyang (alternative birthplace)',
     more: 'Yijing (I-Tsing, 635–713) sailed from Guangzhou in 671 and spent about six months at Srivijaya (Palembang) learning Sanskrit, then reached Tamralipti in 673. He studied at Nalanda for about ten years (c. 675–685) and visited Bodh Gaya. His "Record of the Buddhist Religion as Practised in India and the Malay Archipelago" describes monastic life, Nalanda\'s routine and its many monks. He also wrote biographies of some 60 Chinese pilgrims to India and returned to Luoyang in 695.' } ],
  ['Al-Biruni — Kath (Beruniy)',41.683,60.75,'Uzbekistan (Karakalpakstan)','Born 973 at Kath, Khwarezm; came with Mahmud of Ghazni; wrote Kitab-ul-Hind (Tahqiq-i-Hind).','trav',
   { old: 'Kath / Kat (ancient capital of Khwarezm, Afrighid dynasty) → Shabbaz → Beruniy (renamed 1957)',
     more: 'Abu Rayhan al-Biruni was born in 973 near Kath, the old Khwarezmian capital; the modern town was renamed Beruniy in his honour. After Mahmud of Ghazni conquered Khwarezm (1017), he was taken to Ghazni and accompanied Mahmud\'s campaigns in India, learning Sanskrit. His Kitab fi Tahqiq ma li\'l-Hind (c. 1030) objectively describes Indian philosophy, astronomy, caste and customs, and criticises Indian insularity. He also measured the Earth\'s radius. Died in Ghazni c. 1050.' } ],
  ['Marco Polo & Manucci — Venice',45.437,12.335,'Italy','Marco Polo visited Kakatiya Rudramadevi’s Motupalli (c. 1292); Niccolao Manucci served Dara Shikoh, wrote Storia do Mogor.','trav',
   { old: 'Venetia; Serenissima Repubblica di Venezia; Rialto (early core)',
     more: 'Marco Polo (1254–1324) served Kublai Khan for 17 years and on his return voyage (c. 1292–93) visited Sri Lanka and the Coromandel and Malabar coasts; he praised the Kakatiya queen Rudramadevi\'s kingdom (port Motupalli) and the Pandya pearl fisheries. His Travels was dictated in a Genoese prison. Niccolao Manucci (1638–1717), another Venetian, came to India in 1656, served as gunner for Dara Shikoh, then as physician, and wrote the Storia do Mogor on Mughal history.' } ],
  ['Nicolo Conti — Chioggia',45.22,12.279,'Italy','Venetian merchant from Chioggia; visited Vijayanagara under Deva Raya I (c. 1420).','trav',
   { old: 'Clodia / Fossa Clodia (Roman); Chioggia',
     more: 'Niccolò de\' Conti, a Venetian merchant from Chioggia, travelled through Persia, India and South-East Asia (c. 1419–1444). He visited Vijayanagara (Bizenegalia) around 1420 under Deva Raya I, describing a city 60 miles in circumference, the king\'s many wives and sati, and festivals such as Deepavali. He also described Malabar, Bengal and Java. Forced to convert to Islam, he told his story to the papal secretary Poggio Bracciolini to obtain absolution.' } ],
  ['Ibn Battuta — Tangier',35.777,-5.804,'Morocco','Born 1304; qazi of Delhi under Muhammad bin Tughlaq; sent as envoy to China 1342; Rihla.','trav',
   { old: 'Tingis (Phoenician–Roman, capital of Mauretania Tingitana); Tanja',
     more: 'Ibn Battuta (1304–c. 1368/69), a Moroccan Berber jurist from Tangier, travelled some 120,000 km over about 30 years. He reached Delhi in 1334 and was made qazi by Sultan Muhammad bin Tughlaq, whose cruelty and token-currency and capital-shift experiments he described. In 1342 the sultan sent him as envoy to the Yuan court; he went via Calicut, the Maldives (where he was qazi), Sri Lanka and Bengal. His Rihla, dictated to Ibn Juzayy, describes the barid (postal) system.' } ],
  ['Abdur Razzaq — Herat',34.352,62.204,'Afghanistan','Envoy of Timurid Shah Rukh; visited Calicut (Zamorin) & Vijayanagara under Deva Raya II (1443).','trav',
   { old: 'Haraiva / Aria (Achaemenid); Alexandria Ariana',
     more: 'Kamal-ud-din Abdur Razzaq Samarqandi (1413–1482) was an envoy of the Timurid ruler Shah Rukh of Herat. He reached Calicut in 1442, describing the Zamorin\'s port and its safe trade, then went to Vijayanagara in 1443 during the reign of Deva Raya II, praising the seven fortified walls, markets and the Mahanavami festival: "the eye has never seen a place like it". His account is in his history Matla-us-Sadain wa Majma-ul-Bahrain.' } ],
  ['Afanasy Nikitin — Tver',56.862,35.924,'Russia','Russian merchant in the Bahmani kingdom (Bidar) under Muhammad Shah III, 1469–72; "Journey Beyond Three Seas".','trav',
   { old: 'Tver → Kalinin (1931–1990) → Tver',
     more: 'Afanasy Nikitin, a merchant from the principality of Tver, travelled via the Volga, Persia and Hormuz to India (c. 1469–1472), landing at Chaul. He spent about three years in the Bahmani kingdom, mostly at the capital Bidar under Sultan Muhammad Shah III and minister Mahmud Gawan, describing the wealth of nobles and poverty of peasants. His diary "Khozheniye za tri morya" (Journey Beyond Three Seas) is the first Russian account of India. He died near Smolensk on the way home (c. 1472–75).' } ],
  ['Paes & Barbosa — Lisbon',38.711,-9.139,'Portugal','Domingo Paes visited Vijayanagara under Krishnadeva Raya (c. 1520); Duarte Barbosa served at Cannanore & Cochin.','trav',
   { old: 'Olisipo / Felicitas Julia (Roman); al-Ushbuna (Moorish); Lisboa',
     more: 'Lisbon was the capital of the Portuguese empire whose Estado da India was based at Goa (captured by Albuquerque, 1510). Domingo Paes, a horse trader, visited Vijayanagara c. 1520–22 under Krishnadeva Raya and described the city, the king and the Mahanavami Dibba festivals; Fernão Nunes wrote of its decline c. 1535–37. Duarte Barbosa, a scribe at Cannanore and Cochin (1500–1516), wrote the "Book of Duarte Barbosa" on the Indian Ocean coasts. Vasco da Gama sailed from Lisbon in 1497.' } ],
  ['Tavernier & Bernier — Paris',48.857,2.352,'France','French travellers: Tavernier (jeweller) saw Golconda diamond mines; Bernier, physician at Shah Jahan–Aurangzeb’s court.','trav',
   { old: 'Lutetia Parisiorum (Roman); Paris',
     more: 'Jean-Baptiste Tavernier (1605–1689), a French gem merchant, made six voyages to the East (1630–68), visited the Golconda (Kollur) diamond mines and saw the Peacock Throne; he sold the large blue diamond (later the Hope Diamond) to Louis XIV. François Bernier (1620–1688), a physician, served Dara Shikoh and then Danishmand Khan at Aurangzeb\'s court (1658–70); his "Travels in the Mogul Empire" argued there was no private property in land, influencing Marx\'s "Asiatic mode" idea.' } ],
  ['Sir Thomas Roe — London',51.507,-0.128,'United Kingdom','Ambassador of James I to Jahangir (1615–19) at Ajmer; sought trading rights for the English East India Co.','trav',
   { old: 'Londinium (Roman); Lundenwic (Anglo-Saxon)',
     more: 'Sir Thomas Roe, ambassador of King James I, was at Jahangir\'s court from 1615 to 1619, mostly at Ajmer and Mandu, following Captain William Hawkins\' earlier visit (1608–11). He won concessions for the English East India Company to trade and reside at Surat (a farman from Prince Khurram, 1618), where its factory dated from 1613, and advised the Company to avoid costly forts. His journal describes Jahangir, Nur Jahan and the court. The Company was chartered in London in 1600.' } ]
 ]
});

