GEO.add({
 id:'his-worldhist', group:'his-world', title:'World History Landmarks', bn:'বিশ্ব ইতিহাসের গুরুত্বপূর্ণ স্থান', icon:'🌍', world:true,
 intro:'GS-I বিশ্ব ইতিহাস: আমেরিকান ও ফরাসি বিপ্লব, দুই বিশ্বযুদ্ধ, উপনিবেশ ও চুক্তি, ঠান্ডা যুদ্ধ, জোটনিরপেক্ষ আন্দোলন — প্রতিটি ঘটনার সাল আর স্থান জোড়া করে পড়ো।',
 types:{anc:['Ancient civilisation','#8d6e63'], war:['Revolution / war','#c62828'], treaty:['Treaty / conference / colonial','#1565c0']},
 pts:[
  ['Ur',30.962,46.105,'Iraq','Sumerian city-state; Royal Tombs; ziggurat of Ur-Nammu (c. 2100 BCE); trade with Meluhha (Indus).','anc',
   { old: 'Urim (Sumerian); "Ur of the Chaldees" (Bible); Tell el-Muqayyar (modern mound)',
     more: 'Ur near the old course of the Euphrates was a leading Sumerian city-state. Leonard Woolley\'s excavations (1922–34) revealed the Royal Cemetery (c. 2600 BCE) with the Standard of Ur, gold helmet and lyres. Ur-Nammu of the Third Dynasty of Ur (c. 2100 BCE) built the great ziggurat and issued the oldest surviving law code. Carnelian beads and Indus-type seals point to trade with Meluhha (Harappans). Biblical tradition links Ur with Abraham. UNESCO 2016 (Ahwar of Southern Iraq).' } ],
  ['Babylon',32.543,44.421,'Iraq','Hammurabi’s law code (c. 1754 BCE); Nebuchadnezzar II’s Ishtar Gate; Hanging Gardens (legend); UNESCO.','anc',
   { old: 'Bab-ili ("Gate of God", Akkadian); Babel (Hebrew); Hillah nearby',
     more: 'Babylon on the Euphrates rose under Hammurabi (r. c. 1792–1750 BCE), whose code of 282 laws ("an eye for an eye") is inscribed on a basalt stele now in the Louvre. Nebuchadnezzar II (r. 605–562 BCE) rebuilt it with the blue-glazed Ishtar Gate (now in Berlin), the Etemenanki ziggurat (the "Tower of Babel") and, by tradition, the Hanging Gardens; he exiled the Jews. Cyrus took it in 539 BCE; Alexander died here in 323 BCE. UNESCO 2019.' } ],
  ['Giza Pyramids & Memphis',29.979,31.134,'Egypt','Great Pyramid of Khufu (c. 2560 BCE), Old Kingdom; Sphinx; old capital Memphis lies ~20 km south.','anc',
   { old: 'Memphis = Men-nefer / Ineb-hedj ("White Walls"); Giza necropolis',
     more: 'Memphis, founded by the legendary king Menes c. 3100 BCE, was the capital of Old Kingdom Egypt. At Giza, Khufu (Cheops) built the Great Pyramid (c. 2560 BCE, 146 m), the only surviving wonder of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, followed by the pyramids of Khafre and Menkaure; the Great Sphinx is usually attributed to Khafre. They were royal tombs built by organised labour, not slaves as once believed. Memphis and its Necropolis, UNESCO 1979.' } ],
  ['Thebes — Karnak (Luxor)',25.718,32.658,'Egypt','New Kingdom capital; Karnak & Luxor temples; Valley of the Kings (Tutankhamun) across the Nile.','anc',
   { old: 'Waset (Egyptian); Thebes / Diospolis Magna (Greek); Luxor (from Arabic al-Uqsur, "the palaces")',
     more: 'Thebes was the capital of Egypt in much of the Middle and New Kingdoms (c. 1550–1070 BCE), centre of the cult of Amun-Ra. The Karnak complex, enlarged by pharaohs over 2,000 years, includes the great hypostyle hall of Seti I and Ramesses II; Luxor Temple was built by Amenhotep III and Ramesses II. Across the Nile lie the Valley of the Kings, where Howard Carter found Tutankhamun\'s tomb (1922), and Hatshepsut\'s temple at Deir el-Bahari. UNESCO 1979.' } ],
  ['Knossos',35.298,25.163,'Greece (Crete)','Minoan palace (c. 1900–1450 BCE); Linear A; excavated by Arthur Evans from 1900.','anc',
   { old: 'Ko-no-so (Linear B); Kephala hill',
     more: 'Knossos on Crete was the largest centre of the Bronze Age Minoan civilisation (c. 2000–1450 BCE), linked in Greek myth to King Minos and the Labyrinth of the Minotaur. Arthur Evans excavated and partly reconstructed the palace from 1900, revealing frescoes (bull-leaping), storage jars, drainage and Linear A (undeciphered) and Linear B tablets; Michael Ventris deciphered Linear B as early Greek in 1952. Mycenaean Greeks took over c. 1450 BCE. Minoan Palatial Centres, UNESCO 2025.' } ],
  ['Athens — Acropolis',37.972,23.726,'Greece','Birthplace of democracy (Cleisthenes, 508 BCE); Parthenon built under Pericles (447–432 BCE).','anc',
   { old: 'Athenai (named after Athena); Cecropia (mythical early name)',
     more: 'Cleisthenes\' reforms of 508/507 BCE created Athenian democracy, later expanded under Pericles, though women, slaves and foreigners were excluded. After victory over Persia (Marathon 490, Salamis 480), Athens led the Delian League. Pericles rebuilt the Acropolis, and the Parthenon (447–432 BCE), designed by Ictinus and Callicrates with sculptor Phidias, honoured Athena. Socrates, Plato and Aristotle taught here. Sparta defeated Athens in the Peloponnesian War (404 BCE). UNESCO 1987.' } ],
  ['Persepolis',29.935,52.89,'Iran (Fars)','Achaemenid ceremonial capital of Darius I (from 518 BCE); Indian tribute-bearers on reliefs; burnt by Alexander 330 BCE.','anc',
   { old: 'Parsa (Old Persian); Persepolis ("city of the Persians", Greek); Takht-e Jamshid',
     more: 'Darius I began Persepolis c. 518 BCE as the ceremonial capital of the Achaemenid Empire; Xerxes and Artaxerxes I completed it. The Apadana staircase reliefs show 23 subject peoples bringing tribute, including Indians (Hindush, the Indus satrapy) bringing gold dust. Darius\'s inscriptions (Naqsh-e Rustam) list Gandhara and Hindush as provinces. Alexander the Great burned the palaces in 330 BCE. Old Persian cuneiform tablets survive. UNESCO 1979.' } ],
  ['Susa',32.191,48.258,'Iran (Khuzestan)','Elamite & Achaemenid capital; Hammurabi stele found here (1901); western Royal Road ran to Sardis.','anc',
   { old: 'Shushan (biblical); Susa (Greek); Shush (modern)',
     more: 'Susa was the capital of the Elamite civilisation from c. 4000 BCE and later one of the Achaemenid capitals, the starting point of the Royal Road to Sardis described by Herodotus. Darius I built a palace here; its foundation charter says materials came from as far as Gandhara and Sind (teak, ivory). French excavators found the Code of Hammurabi stele here in 1901–02, carried off by an Elamite king as plunder. Alexander held the mass marriage of Greeks and Persians at Susa (324 BCE). UNESCO 2015.' } ],
  ['Anyang — Yinxu',36.123,114.319,'China (Henan)','Late Shang capital (c. 1300–1046 BCE); oracle-bone inscriptions, earliest Chinese writing; UNESCO.','anc',
   { old: 'Yin / Yinxu ("Ruins of Yin"), last Shang capital; Dayi Shang',
     more: 'Yinxu near Anyang was the capital of the late Shang dynasty from about the reign of Pan Geng (c. 1300 BCE) until the Zhou conquest (c. 1046 BCE). Oracle bones (turtle shells and ox scapulae) bear the earliest systematic Chinese writing, used for royal divination; they were identified in 1899. Excavations since 1928 found royal tombs, chariot burials and bronzes, including the tomb of Fu Hao, a queen and general. UNESCO 2006.' } ],
  ['Teotihuacan',19.692,-98.844,'Mexico','Pre-Aztec city (c. 100 BCE–550 CE); Pyramids of the Sun & Moon; Avenue of the Dead; UNESCO.','anc',
   { old: 'Teotihuacan = "place where the gods were created" (Aztec Nahuatl name; original name unknown)',
     more: 'Teotihuacan in the Valley of Mexico was the largest city of the pre-Columbian Americas at its peak (c. 100–550 CE, perhaps 100,000–125,000 people). Its grid plan centres on the Avenue of the Dead with the Pyramid of the Sun, the Pyramid of the Moon and the Temple of the Feathered Serpent (Quetzalcoatl). The city was burned and abandoned c. 550–650 CE; the Aztecs later revered the ruins and gave them their name. UNESCO 1987.' } ],
  ['Machu Picchu',-13.163,-72.546,'Peru','15th-c. Inca citadel (Pachacuti); brought to world notice by Hiram Bingham, 1911; UNESCO.','anc',
   { old: 'Machu Picchu = "old peak" (Quechua); original Inca name possibly Huayna Picchu or Picchu (debated)',
     more: 'Machu Picchu, on a ridge above the Urubamba valley at about 2,430 m, was built c. 1450 as an estate of the Inca emperor Pachacuti. Its dry-stone (ashlar) masonry, terraces, the Temple of the Sun and the Intihuatana stone show Inca engineering. It was abandoned about the time of the Spanish conquest (1530s) and escaped Spanish notice. Yale\'s Hiram Bingham publicised it in 1911. The Inca road network (Qhapaq Ñan) is also UNESCO-listed. UNESCO 1983; New Seven Wonders 2007.' } ],
  ['Münster — Peace of Westphalia (1648)',51.962,7.626,'Germany','Treaties ending the Thirty Years’ War (1648); basis of the modern sovereign nation-state system.','treaty',
   { old: 'Mimigernaford (early settlement); Monasterium (after the monastery of Liudger, c. 793)',
     more: 'The Peace of Westphalia (1648) consisted of treaties signed at Münster (Catholic powers) and Osnabrück (Protestant powers), ending the Thirty Years\' War in the Holy Roman Empire and the Eighty Years\' War (Dutch independence recognised by Spain). The principle of cuius regio, eius religio was reaffirmed and extended to Calvinists; Switzerland and the Netherlands were recognised. It is often cited as the origin of the modern system of sovereign states (Westphalian sovereignty). Signed in Münster\'s Hall of Peace.' } ],
  ['Lexington & Concord (1775)',42.449,-71.231,'USA (Massachusetts)','First shots of the American War of Independence, 19 Apr 1775.','war',
   { old: 'Cambridge Farms (early Lexington); Musketaquid (Native name of Concord)',
     more: 'On 19 April 1775, British troops from Boston marched to seize colonial militia arms at Concord. Militiamen met them at Lexington Green, where the first shots were fired, and at Concord\'s North Bridge — Emerson\'s "shot heard round the world". Paul Revere and William Dawes had ridden to warn the colonists. The British retreated to Boston under fire, beginning the American War of Independence (1775–83), which ended with the Treaty of Paris (1783).' } ],
  ['Philadelphia — Independence Hall',39.949,-75.15,'USA (Pennsylvania)','Declaration of Independence adopted 4 July 1776; US Constitution drafted here 1787.','war',
   { old: 'Pennsylvania State House (Independence Hall\'s original name); Philadelphia = "brotherly love" (Penn, 1682)',
     more: 'Founded by William Penn in 1682, Philadelphia hosted the Continental Congress. In the State House (Independence Hall) the Declaration of Independence, drafted mainly by Thomas Jefferson, was adopted on 4 July 1776, stating that "all men are created equal". In 1787 the Constitutional Convention here drafted the US Constitution, with federalism, separation of powers and checks and balances (the Bill of Rights followed in 1791). The Liberty Bell is nearby. UNESCO 1979.' } ],
  ['Paris — Bastille (1789)',48.853,2.369,'France','Storming of the Bastille, 14 July 1789 — start of the French Revolution (Liberty, Equality, Fraternity).','war',
   { old: 'Bastille Saint-Antoine (fortress, 1370s–1789); now Place de la Bastille',
     more: 'The Bastille was a medieval fortress used as a royal prison, a symbol of absolutist rule under the Bourbons. On 14 July 1789, Parisian crowds seeking gunpowder stormed it and killed its governor de Launay; it was demolished soon after. The Estates-General had become the National Assembly (Tennis Court Oath, June 1789), and the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen followed in August. 14 July is French National Day. The July Column (1830 revolution) stands on the site.' } ],
  ['Waterloo (1815)',50.678,4.405,'Belgium','Napoleon finally defeated by Wellington & Blücher, 18 June 1815 (Lion’s Mound marks the field).','war',
   { old: 'Battle of Mont-Saint-Jean / La Belle Alliance (French & Prussian names)',
     more: 'On 18 June 1815, after his escape from Elba and the "Hundred Days", Napoleon was defeated south of Brussels by the Anglo-allied army of the Duke of Wellington and the Prussian army of Gebhard von Blücher. He abdicated again and was exiled to St Helena, where he died in 1821. The Congress of Vienna had already been redrawing Europe. The Lion\'s Mound (1826) marks where the Prince of Orange was wounded. "Meeting one\'s Waterloo" means a final defeat.' } ],
  ['Vienna — Hofburg (1815)',48.206,16.365,'Austria','Congress of Vienna (1814–15) under Metternich: restored monarchies & balance of power after Napoleon.','treaty',
   { old: 'Vindobona (Roman camp); Wenia (medieval); Wien',
     more: 'The Congress of Vienna (September 1814–June 1815), chaired by Austrian chancellor Klemens von Metternich and held largely in the Habsburg Hofburg palace, redrew the map of Europe after Napoleon. Guided by legitimacy and the balance of power, it restored the Bourbons, created the German Confederation, and enlarged Prussia and Russia (Congress Poland). It was followed by the Concert of Europe and the conservative Holy Alliance. It also condemned the slave trade and set diplomatic ranks.' } ],
  ['Nanjing — Treaty of 1842',32.09,118.74,'China (Jiangsu)','Treaty of Nanking ended the First Opium War: Hong Kong ceded to Britain, five treaty ports opened.','treaty',
   { old: 'Jinling; Jianye / Jiankang (Six Dynasties); Nanking ("southern capital", Ming); Tianjing (Taiping capital)',
     more: 'The Treaty of Nanking, signed on 29 August 1842 aboard HMS Cornwallis near Nanjing, ended the First Opium War (1839–42), triggered when Commissioner Lin Zexu destroyed British opium from India. China ceded Hong Kong Island, opened five treaty ports (Canton, Amoy, Foochow, Ningpo, Shanghai) and paid an indemnity — the first "unequal treaty". Nanjing was later the Taiping capital (1853–64) and site of the Japanese massacre (1937).' } ],
  ['Tokyo — Imperial Palace (Meiji)',35.682,139.753,'Japan','Meiji Restoration 1868: emperor moved from Kyoto to Edo, renamed Tokyo; rapid modernisation.','war',
   { old: 'Edo (Tokugawa shogunate seat from 1603) → Tokyo ("eastern capital", 1868)',
     more: 'Edo was the seat of the Tokugawa shoguns. After the US Commodore Perry forced Japan open (1853–54), the shogunate was overthrown and power restored to the young Emperor Meiji in 1868. The capital moved from Kyoto to Edo, renamed Tokyo, and the imperial family moved into Edo Castle, now the Imperial Palace. The Meiji government abolished feudal domains (1871), introduced conscription and industrialisation, and adopted the Meiji Constitution (1889) on a Prussian model.' } ],
  ['Shimonoseki — Treaty of 1895',33.957,130.94,'Japan','Ended the First Sino-Japanese War: China ceded Taiwan, recognised Korean independence.','treaty',
   { old: 'Akamagaseki / Bakan (old names)',
     more: 'The Treaty of Shimonoseki, signed on 17 April 1895 by Li Hongzhang and Itō Hirobumi, ended the First Sino-Japanese War over Korea. China recognised Korea\'s independence, ceded Taiwan, the Pescadores and the Liaodong peninsula to Japan, and paid a large indemnity. Russia, Germany and France forced Japan to return Liaodong (Triple Intervention), leading to the Russo-Japanese War (1904–05). Shimonoseki strait was also the scene of the Genpei War\'s Battle of Dan-no-ura (1185).' } ],
  ['Berlin — Brandenburg Gate',52.516,13.378,'Germany','Berlin Conference 1884–85 partitioned Africa; Berlin Wall 1961–89 (fell 9 Nov 1989); reunification 1990.','treaty',
   { old: 'Berlin & Cölln (twin medieval towns); East / West Berlin (1949–90)',
     more: 'The neoclassical Brandenburg Gate (1791) was built for Prussian king Frederick William II. The Berlin Conference (1884–85), hosted by Bismarck, set rules for the European "Scramble for Africa" (effective occupation) and recognised the Congo Free State of Leopold II. After WWII Berlin was divided; the Berlin Blockade (1948–49) and the Wall (from 13 August 1961) symbolised the Cold War. The Wall fell on 9 November 1989 and Germany reunified on 3 October 1990.' } ],
  ['Sarajevo — Latin Bridge (1914)',43.858,18.429,'Bosnia & Herzegovina','Archduke Franz Ferdinand shot by Gavrilo Princip, 28 June 1914 — trigger of World War I.','war',
   { old: 'Vrhbosna (medieval); Bosna-Saray ("palace", Ottoman 15th c.); Latin Bridge = Principov most (Princip Bridge, Yugoslav era)',
     more: 'On 28 June 1914, the Bosnian Serb nationalist Gavrilo Princip, of the Young Bosnia group armed by the Serbian "Black Hand", shot Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to Austria-Hungary, and his wife Sophie near the Latin Bridge. Austria-Hungary\'s ultimatum to Serbia and the alliance system (Russia, Germany, France, Britain) turned the crisis into World War I within weeks (July Crisis). Austria had annexed Bosnia in 1908. Sarajevo was besieged in 1992–96.' } ],
  ['Verdun (1916)',49.16,5.384,'France','Longest battle of WWI (Feb–Dec 1916): France held the German offensive at enormous cost.','war',
   { old: 'Verodunum (Gallo-Roman); Treaty of Verdun (843) divided Charlemagne\'s empire',
     more: 'The Battle of Verdun (21 February–18 December 1916) was a German offensive under Erich von Falkenhayn meant to "bleed France white" by attacking a fortress city France would defend at any cost. Under Philippe Pétain ("They shall not pass") the French held, supplied via the Voie Sacrée road. There were about 700,000 casualties in total, making it a symbol of WWI attrition warfare. The Treaty of Verdun (843) had earlier divided the Carolingian empire into three.' } ],
  ['Somme — Thiepval (1916)',50.051,2.686,'France','Anglo-French offensive Jul–Nov 1916; ~57,000 British casualties on day one; tanks first used (Sept 1916).','war',
   { old: 'Somme (river and département); Thiepval Memorial to the Missing (1932)',
     more: 'The Battle of the Somme (1 July–18 November 1916) was an Anglo-French offensive, partly to relieve Verdun. On the first day the British suffered about 57,000 casualties (about 19,000 killed), the worst day in British military history. Tanks were first used at Flers–Courcelette on 15 September 1916. Indian cavalry units fought here. Total casualties exceeded a million for modest gains. The Thiepval Memorial lists over 72,000 missing British and South African soldiers.' } ],
  ['Petrograd — Winter Palace (1917)',59.94,30.314,'Russia (St Petersburg)','Bolsheviks under Lenin seize the Winter Palace, Oct (Nov) 1917 — the October Revolution.','war',
   { old: 'Saint Petersburg (1703) → Petrograd (1914) → Leningrad (1924) → Saint Petersburg (1991)',
     more: 'In the February Revolution (March 1917, new style) Tsar Nicholas II abdicated and a Provisional Government took power. Lenin returned from exile and issued the April Theses ("Peace, Land, Bread"). On 25 October (7 November N.S.) 1917, Bolshevik Red Guards organised by Trotsky\'s Military Revolutionary Committee stormed the Winter Palace, the former imperial residence, arresting the Provisional Government. Russia left WWI by the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk (1918). The palace now houses the Hermitage Museum.' } ],
  ['Versailles (1919)',48.805,2.12,'France','Treaty of Versailles signed in the Hall of Mirrors, 28 June 1919, ending WWI; German Empire proclaimed here 1871.','treaty',
   { old: 'Versailles (hunting lodge of Louis XIII → royal palace of Louis XIV, 1682)',
     more: 'Louis XIV moved the French court to Versailles in 1682. The German Empire was proclaimed in its Hall of Mirrors on 18 January 1871 after Prussia\'s victory over France; in the same hall the Treaty of Versailles was signed on 28 June 1919. It imposed the "war guilt" clause (Article 231), reparations, loss of Alsace-Lorraine and colonies, and army limits on Germany, and created the League of Nations. India signed separately as a member of the British Empire delegation. UNESCO 1979.' } ],
  ['Pearl Harbor (1941)',21.368,-157.977,'USA (Hawaii)','Japanese attack on the US Pacific Fleet, 7 Dec 1941; USA enters World War II.','war',
   { old: 'Wai Momi ("waters of pearl", Hawaiian); Puʻuloa',
     more: 'On the morning of 7 December 1941, Japanese carrier aircraft under Admiral Yamamoto\'s plan attacked the US Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor on Oahu, sinking or damaging eight battleships and killing about 2,400 Americans; the US aircraft carriers were absent. President Roosevelt called it "a date which will live in infamy". The US declared war on Japan the next day, and Germany declared war on the US on 11 December. The USS Arizona Memorial marks the sunken battleship.' } ],
  ['Stalingrad — Mamayev Kurgan',48.743,44.537,'Russia (Volgograd)','Soviet victory over the German 6th Army (Aug 1942–Feb 1943) — turning point of WWII in Europe.','war',
   { old: 'Tsaritsyn (to 1925) → Stalingrad (1925–61) → Volgograd (1961)',
     more: 'From August 1942 the German 6th Army under Friedrich Paulus fought street by street for Stalingrad on the Volga. In November the Soviets launched Operation Uranus under Zhukov and Vasilevsky, encircling the Germans; Paulus surrendered on 2 February 1943. With about 2 million total casualties it was among the bloodiest battles in history and the turning point on the Eastern Front. The hill of Mamayev Kurgan, heavily fought over, bears the 85 m statue "The Motherland Calls".' } ],
  ['Normandy — Omaha Beach (1944)',49.369,-0.869,'France','D-Day, 6 June 1944: Allied landings (Operation Overlord) open the Western Front.','war',
   { old: 'Neustria (Frankish); Normandy = land of the Northmen (Rollo, 911)',
     more: 'On D-Day, 6 June 1944, Operation Overlord, commanded by General Dwight Eisenhower, landed about 156,000 Allied troops on five Normandy beaches (Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno, Sword) after airborne drops, the largest seaborne invasion in history. US forces suffered heavy losses at Omaha Beach. The landings opened the Western Front demanded by Stalin; Paris was liberated in August 1944 and Germany surrendered on 7–8 May 1945.' } ],
  ['Yalta — Livadia Palace (1945)',44.468,34.143,'Crimea','Feb 1945: Roosevelt, Churchill & Stalin plan post-war Europe and the UN; Soviet entry against Japan.','treaty',
   { old: 'Livadia = Greek "meadow" (Romanov estate); Yalta in Crimea',
     more: 'The Yalta (Crimea) Conference of 4–11 February 1945 was held in Livadia Palace, the summer palace of Nicholas II. Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin agreed on Germany\'s division into occupation zones, free elections in Eastern Europe (the "Declaration on Liberated Europe"), Poland\'s borders, and Soviet entry into the war against Japan within three months of Germany\'s defeat. They agreed on UN Security Council voting (veto) and called the San Francisco Conference (April 1945).' } ],
  ['Potsdam — Cecilienhof (1945)',52.419,13.071,'Germany','Jul–Aug 1945: Truman, Stalin, Churchill/Attlee; Potsdam Declaration demands Japan’s surrender.','treaty',
   { old: 'Poztupimi (Slavic, 993 charter); Cecilienhof (palace of Crown Princess Cecilie, 1917)',
     more: 'The Potsdam Conference (17 July–2 August 1945) in the Cecilienhof palace brought together Truman, Stalin and Churchill, who was replaced by Clement Attlee after the British election. They agreed on the "four Ds" for Germany (demilitarisation, denazification, democratisation, decentralisation), reparations and the Oder–Neisse line for Poland. The Potsdam Declaration (26 July) demanded Japan\'s unconditional surrender or "prompt and utter destruction". Palaces of Potsdam, UNESCO 1990.' } ],
  ['Hiroshima (1945)',34.396,132.454,'Japan','First atomic bomb ("Little Boy"), 6 Aug 1945; Genbaku Dome is a UNESCO site.','war',
   { old: 'Hiroshima (castle town of Mōri Terumoto, 1589); Genbaku Dome = Hiroshima Prefectural Industrial Promotion Hall',
     more: 'On 6 August 1945 the US B-29 bomber Enola Gay dropped the uranium bomb "Little Boy" on Hiroshima, killing about 70,000–80,000 people at once and perhaps 140,000 by the end of 1945. The ruined Prefectural Industrial Promotion Hall, near the hypocentre, was preserved as the Genbaku (Atomic Bomb) Dome, a UNESCO site since 1996. The bombing shaped the nuclear disarmament debate (NPT 1968, TPNW 2017); 6 August is observed as Hiroshima Day.' } ],
  ['Nagasaki (1945)',32.776,129.863,'Japan','Second atomic bomb ("Fat Man"), 9 Aug 1945; Japan announced surrender 15 Aug 1945.','war',
   { old: 'Fukae-no-ura / Tamano-ura (old names); Nagasaki = "long cape"',
     more: 'Nagasaki was Japan\'s window to Europe during the Tokugawa isolation, when only the Dutch could trade at the artificial island of Dejima. On 9 August 1945 the plutonium bomb "Fat Man" exploded over the Urakami valley, killing about 40,000 at once and about 70,000 by year end; the primary target, Kokura, was clouded. The Soviet Union had declared war on Japan on 8 August. Emperor Hirohito announced surrender on 15 August; the formal signing on USS Missouri was on 2 September 1945.' } ],
  ['Beijing — Tiananmen',39.903,116.392,'China','May Fourth Movement 1919; Mao proclaimed the PRC here, 1 Oct 1949; 1989 protests crushed.','war',
   { old: 'Chengtianmen (Ming gate, 1420) → Tiananmen ("Gate of Heavenly Peace", 1651)',
     more: 'Tiananmen is the gate in front of the Forbidden City. Student protests here on 4 May 1919 against the Treaty of Versailles transferring Shandong to Japan began the May Fourth Movement of Chinese nationalism. Mao Zedong proclaimed the People\'s Republic of China from the gate on 1 October 1949. Tiananmen Square later hosted Red Guard rallies of the Cultural Revolution. In June 1989, pro-democracy protests were crushed by the army.' } ],
  ['Dien Bien Phu (1954)',21.383,103.017,'Vietnam','Viet Minh under Giap defeat the French, May 1954; Geneva Accords divide Vietnam at the 17th parallel.','war',
   { old: 'Mường Thanh (Thai name of the valley); Điện Biên Phủ = "frontier prefecture seat"',
     more: 'In 1953 the French set up a fortified base in the Dien Bien Phu valley near Laos to lure the Viet Minh into battle. General Võ Nguyên Giáp\'s forces hauled artillery over the hills by hand, surrounded the garrison and captured it on 7 May 1954 after a 56-day siege. The defeat ended French rule in Indochina. The Geneva Conference (1954), co-chaired by Britain and the USSR with India active, divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel; India chaired the International Control Commission.' } ],
  ['Bandung — Gedung Merdeka (1955)',-6.921,107.609,'Indonesia','Asian-African Conference, Apr 1955 (Nehru, Sukarno, Nasser, Zhou Enlai); Dasasila; roots of NAM.','treaty',
   { old: 'Gedung Merdeka = former Societeit Concordia (Dutch club, 1895); Bandoeng (Dutch spelling)',
     more: 'The Asian-African Conference of 18–24 April 1955, held in the Gedung Merdeka (renamed "Freedom Building" for it), brought together 29 newly independent countries, convened by Indonesia, India, Pakistan, Burma and Ceylon (the Colombo Powers). Nehru, Sukarno, Nasser, Zhou Enlai and U Nu attended. Its Ten Principles (Dasasila Bandung) incorporated India\'s Panchsheel and opposed colonialism, laying the groundwork for the Non-Aligned Movement (1961).' } ],
  ['Port Said — Suez Crisis (1956)',31.263,32.306,'Egypt','Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal (July 1956); Anglo-French-Israeli attack, Port Said landings; first UNEF.','war',
   { old: 'Port Said, named after Khedive Said Pasha (founded 1859 with canal works); near ancient Pelusium',
     more: 'Port Said was founded in 1859 at the northern entrance of the Suez Canal, opened in 1869 by Ferdinand de Lesseps. On 26 July 1956 President Nasser nationalised the canal company after the US and Britain withdrew funding for the Aswan High Dam. Israel invaded Sinai and Anglo-French forces landed at Port Said (November 1956), but US and Soviet pressure forced withdrawal. The first UN peacekeeping force (UNEF) was deployed; India contributed troops. It marked Britain\'s decline as a world power.' } ],
  ['Belgrade — NAM Summit (1961)',44.812,20.466,'Serbia','First Non-Aligned Movement summit, Sept 1961 — Tito, Nehru, Nasser, Sukarno, Nkrumah; 25 members.','treaty',
   { old: 'Singidunum (Celtic–Roman); Beograd ("white city"); Nándorfehérvár (Hungarian)',
     more: 'The first summit of the Non-Aligned Movement was held in Belgrade, capital of Yugoslavia, on 1–6 September 1961, hosted by Josip Broz Tito. Its founders were Tito, Nehru, Nasser, Sukarno and Kwame Nkrumah; 25 countries attended as full members. NAM rejected joining either Cold War bloc and supported decolonisation and disarmament; the Brioni meeting (1956) had prepared it. India hosted the 7th NAM summit in New Delhi (1983). NAM now has about 120 members.' } ],
  ['Bay of Pigs — Playa Girón (1961)',22.067,-81.033,'Cuba','Failed CIA-backed exile invasion against Castro, Apr 1961; prelude to the Cuban Missile Crisis (1962).','war',
   { old: 'Bahía de Cochinos (Spanish name of the bay); Playa Girón (main landing beach)',
     more: 'On 17 April 1961, about 1,400 CIA-trained Cuban exiles (Brigade 2506) landed at the Bay of Pigs to overthrow Fidel Castro\'s revolutionary government; President Kennedy refused direct US air support and the invaders were defeated within three days. The fiasco pushed Castro closer to the USSR, leading to the deployment of Soviet missiles and the Cuban Missile Crisis of October 1962, resolved when Khrushchev withdrew them and the US pledged not to invade Cuba.' } ]
 ]
});
